      ],
      "postEditingUsage": "사용함",
      "postEditingDetails": "없음",
      "promptSummary": "",
      "fullPrompt": "The pair of gold crown ornaments of Baekje Queen Muryeong (National Treasure). Use it as the exact design source for the sunglasses frame: the silhouette, flame tongues, stacked flowers, vine scrolls, base loop and gold surface.\n\nHigh-fashion editorial campaign photograph, contemporary couture runway still, 4K, luxurious and dramatic, designed for a large LED media facade.\n\nMAIN SUBJECT: A gorgeous Korean female model in her mid-20s with a composed expression walks straight toward the camera mid-stride. Her glossy black hair is pulled back into a sleek low bun.\n\nShe wears avant-garde couture sunglasses whose entire frame IS the pair of ornaments, scaled down and mirrored, one ornament standing upright over each eye:\n- Each frame half keeps the tall pointed flame-shaped outline from mid-cheek to mid-forehead.\n- A dark smoked lens is set into the middle of each ornament, so the gold flowers and scrolls overlap the lens edges.\n\nNo other head ornaments. On one wrist is a chunky polished silver cuff bracelet with a coiled dragon in relief.\n\nHer outfit is sharp, minimal contemporary tailoring that quietly references Baekje dress:\n- Jacket: an oversized hip-length wrap blazer in deep vermilion wool-silk with squared shoulders and a crossed, buttonless front. Wide midnight-indigo bands edge the collar, front, cuffs and hem with gold piping, after Baekje jacket trim.\n- Inner layer: a fitted ivory silk high-neck top.\n- Trousers: high-waisted ivory wide-leg trousers in fluid crepe with sharp creases, falling to the floor, echoing Baekje wide trousers.\n- Belt: a slim black leather belt with small gilt-bronze openwork plaques inspired by Baekje belt ornaments.\n- Fabric: solid colors, only a faint tone-on-tone woven lotus on the blazer.\nShoes: pointed-toe gold dress shoes inspired by Baekje gilt-bronze shoes, polished metallic leather with delicate side openwork, slim heels, no spikes.\n\nENVIRONMENT: A reimagined, monumental brick burial chamber of King Muryeong's tomb in Gongju: a long barrel-vaulted tunnel of warm umber bricks stamped with small lotus flowers, laid in alternating horizontal and vertical courses. Arched wall niches hold white ceramic oil lamps with living flames, repeating like runway lights. A black mirror floor reflects her. A golden haze glows at the far end.\n\nCOMPOSITION: Symmetrical one-point perspective down the runway. The camera is at hip height with a slight upward tilt. The model is centered, full-length, filling 75% of the frame height. The lamp niches converge behind her head, so the gold eyewear glows against the dark vault. The eyewear and face must read clearly from 30m away.\n\nCAMERA AND LIGHTING: 85mm lens at f/2.8. The model is tack sharp and the far vault falls into gentle bokeh. A large soft warm key light from high front sculpts the face and tailoring. The lamp flames give warm 2200K side glow, sparkling through the openwork flowers of the frame and reflecting as small points on the smoked lenses. There is high contrast between the glowing gold and the dark vault, with no crushed blacks.\n\nCOLOR PALETTE: Deep vermilion; midnight indigo; ivory; 24k hammered gold; polished silver; warm umber brick; black floor; amber accents.\n\nMATERIALS AND TEXTURES: Thin hammered sheet gold with tiny tool marks, punched dots and crisp cut-out edges; engraved silver; structured wool-silk; fluid crepe; leather with gilt-bronze plaques; fired lotus-stamped bricks; realistic skin with natural pores and a subtle red lip.\n\nON-IMAGE TEXT: None. No text, logos or watermarks.\n\nCONSTRAINTS: The eyewear frame must reproduce the @img1 ornament design, not a generic ornate, baroque, butterfly, wing or leaf frame. The look must feel like modern Korean couture, not a historical, Chinese or Japanese costume. No neon or hologram effects. The pose must be a real runway walk.\n\nThen change the background to the barrel-vaulted brick tomb chamber of King Muryeong in Gongju, extremely distorted into a colossal spiral vortex. The vault twists around the model and becomes the coiling body of a giant Baekje blue dragon, modeled on the slender, flowing blue dragon of the Four Guardians wall painting in Songsan-ri Tomb No. 6 in Gongju and the dragon on King Muryeong's ring-pommel sword:\n- a long, lean serpentine body in graceful S-curves, with slender limbs and an elegant narrow head with a long snout, an open mouth, and a single backward-sweeping horn with a flame-like mane\n- its scales made of warm umber tomb bricks, each stamped with a small lotus flower\n- its spine ridge, fins and mane made of thin hammered gold openwork flame tongues and honeysuckle vine scrolls.\n- faded mineral blue-green pigment on the brick scales, like an ancient wall painting\nThe body spirals from the floor around and above her, into a vanishing point behind her head, where the dragon's head emerges in the vortex, calm, looking down. The runway is the dragon's back, a curving path of lotus bricks spiraling toward the camera. White ceramic oil lamps with living flames from the tomb's wall niches ride along the spiral, streaking into light trails.",
