      "baekjeRelated": "사용함",
      "description": "공주 의당 집터다지기는 여러 사람이 힘을 모아 다짐돌을 들어 올리고 내리치며 집터를 단단하게 다지는 공동체의 무형유산이다. 본 작품은 이러한 전통의 움직임을 과거와 현재를 연결하는 시간의 순간으로 재해석하였다.\n\n화면 전체에는 현대의 도시 풍경을 배치하고, 그 한가운데 거대한 화면이 찢어진 듯한 형태로 조선시대의 풍경을 드러냈다. 찢어진 틈 안에는 한옥과 산, 들판 등 전통적인 풍경이 펼쳐지고, 흰색 바지와 저고리, 파란 쾌자를 입은 농민이 그 안에서 다짐돌을 향해 뛰어내린다. 다짐돌이 착지하는 순간은 과거와 현재가 맞닿는 시간의 경계가 된다. 착지점에서는 찬란한 네온 빛의 파동과 황금빛 디지털 입자가 사방으로 퍼져나가며, 조선의 풍경에서 현대의 도시로 이어지는 강렬한 시각적 전환을 만든다.\n\n특히 두 시대를 자연스럽게 그라데이션으로 섞는 대신, 한 시대의 화면이 물리적으로 찢어져 다른 시대가 드러나는 시간의 표현을 통해 전통과 현대의 대비를 강조했다. 이는 과거의 유산이 박제된 기억으로 남아 있는 것이 아니라, 현재의 삶 속에서 새롭게 발견되고 이어지는 가치임을 표현한 것이다.\n\n다짐돌의 힘찬 움직임과 착지의 순간을 통해 공동체의 힘으로 터를 다졌던 의당 집터다지기의 정신이 오늘날의 디지털 기술과 만나 새로운 문화적 풍경으로 확장되는 모습을 시각화하였다. 전통의 흔적이 현대를 가르는 경계가 아니라, 과거와 미래를 연결하는 출발점이 되기를 바라는 의미를 담았다.",
      "aiTools": [
        "Magnific - Seedream 5 Lite"
      ],
      "postEditingUsage": "사용함",
      "postEditingDetails": "없음",
      "promptSummary": "",
      "fullPrompt": "The intangible heritage of Uidang Jipteodajigi (Ground-Tamping Ceremony in Uidang, Gongju), reimagined as a sophisticated Korean digital heritage poster.\n<p>\nA SINGLE LARGE, WIDE, LOW, FLAT NATURAL STONE is used as the traditional tamping stone. It is NOT a dolmen, NOT a monument, NOT a standing stone, and NOT a stone table. It is simply one heavy, broad, horizontally positioned natural rock with an irregular natural shape.\n<p>\nThe stone is tightly wrapped in traditional white cotton cloth and bound with thick braided dong-a rope. Multiple long ropes are attached directly around the stone. Twenty Joseon-era Korean farmers wearing white pants and white jeogori pull the long ropes together, lifting the heavy stone and striking it down onto the ground to compact the earth.\n<p>\nOne farmer wearing white pants, a white jeogori and a vivid blue kwaeja jumps through the air and lands directly on the top surface of the same wrapped tamping stone.\n<p>\nIMPORTANT VISUAL COMPOSITION:\n<p>\nThe ENTIRE BACKGROUND is a contemporary Korean city: enormous modern skyscrapers, glass buildings, urban streets, digital billboards, illuminated windows and futuristic metropolitan architecture.\n<p>\nInside this modern city, there is ONE LARGE, IRREGULAR, TORN-OPEN SILHOUETTE that reveals the world of Joseon.\n<p>\nThe Joseon-era landscape is NOT blended into the city.\nIt is NOT a gradient.\nIt is NOT a smooth transition.\nIt is NOT a gradual color change.\n<p>\nInstead, the Joseon landscape appears as if a huge section of the modern world has been PHYSICALLY TORN OPEN.\n<p>\nThe boundary between the two eras must look like a RIPPED PIECE OF PAPER or a TORN FRAGMENT OF AN ANCIENT PAINTING.\n<p>\nThe torn edge is extremely irregular, organic and jagged, with sharp fragments, uneven paper fibers and rough broken contours. The modern city remains clearly visible outside the torn opening, while the entire interior of the torn silhouette contains ONLY the Joseon-era world.\n<p>\nInside the torn opening: traditional Korean hanok, tiled roofs, wooden gates, rural fields, mountains, trees, dirt paths and a Joseon-era village rendered like a traditional Korean landscape painting.\n<p>\nThe torn Joseon landscape extends vertically from the upper part of the composition downward toward the exact location where the blue-kwaeja farmer lands on the tamping stone.\n<p>\nThe farmer appears to be jumping OUT OF the torn Joseon landscape toward the stone.\n<p>\nThe edge of the torn historical landscape should PASS DIRECTLY BEHIND AND AROUND THE FARMER'S TRAJECTORY, making the jump visually connect the historical world and the physical stone.\n<p>\nThe exact landing point of the stone is the sharpest and most dramatic point of the temporal boundary.\n<p>\nWhen the farmer lands on the wrapped stone, brilliant neon light waves and thousands of tiny golden digital particles explode outward from the impact point.\n<p>\nThe neon light and golden particles should NOT create a gradient between the two eras. Instead, they should emphasize the SHARP, TORN BOUNDARY between the Joseon landscape and the modern city.\n<p>\nThe visual idea is:\nMODERN CITY = the present-day world surrounding the image.\nTORN JOSEON LANDSCAPE = a fragment of the past physically revealed inside the present.\nTAMPING STONE = the physical meeting point of the two eras.\nIMPACT = a burst of digital energy connecting heritage and contemporary Korea.\n<p>\nThe composition should strongly resemble a sophisticated surreal heritage poster using a TORN-PAPER COLLAGE principle: one historical world appears through a large irregular tear in another world.\n<p>\nTraditional Korean landscape painting fused with contemporary digital media art, aged Korean paper texture, ink-wash mountains, intricate Joseon architecture, deep navy negative space, antique gold, muted blue and vermilion, contrasted with luminous cyan neon and golden digital particles.\n<p>\nElegant asymmetrical composition, dramatic diagonal trajectory, monumental architecture, tiny human figures, strong negative space, museum-quality visual identity, premium digital heritage festival poster, poetic surrealism.\n<p>\nVERTICAL POSTER COMPOSITION.\n<p>\nCRITICAL: The boundary between the two eras must be a HARD, PHYSICAL, IRREGULAR TORN EDGE. The two worlds must remain visually distinct. No smooth blending, no gradient transition, no atmospheric dissolve, no morphing landscape, no soft fade.\n<p>\nABSOLUTELY NO DOLMEN, NO MEGALITHIC MONUMENT, NO STONEHENGE, NO STANDING STONE, NO STONE TABLE, NO STONE PILLARS, NO STACKED STONES.",
