      "fullPrompt": "Create a complete 30-second, 16:9 Japanese theatrical hand-drawn 2D cel-animation sequence. This prompt contains all instructions for independent generation.\n\nSTORY:\nBegin with <<<image_1>>> sleeping belly-down on the hillside and all four cats from <<<image_4>>> asleep nearby. The brown tabby from <<<image_4>>> wakes first and gently taps <<<image_1>>>'s foot. <<<image_1>>> and the other cats from <<<image_4>>> wake, get up and eagerly set off on patrol. The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> climbs onto <<<image_1>>>'s head as their little lookout. They happily descend to the village entrance.\n\nREFERENCE ASSIGNMENT:\n<<<image_1>>> = the exact Jinmyosu character.\n<<<image_2>>> = the hand-painted Gongju hillside, descending path and sky.\n<<<image_3>>> = the hand-painted present-day Gongju residential alley.\n<<<image_4>>> = the same four cats: brown tabby, calico, black-and-white tuxedo and ginger.\n\nPreserve the exact reference designs, proportions, colors and markings. There is one <<<image_1>>> guardian and exactly four cats from <<<image_4>>>. Select the specified cat from <<<image_4>>> for close shots; do not reproduce the entire group in every close-up. Never display the reference layout.\n\nPreserve <<<image_1>>>'s squat gray-green stone body, short thick legs, round eyes, broad muzzle, back ridges and flame reliefs. The one compact dark iron horn must match <<<image_1>>> exactly in shape, size, attachment and perspective. The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> may sit behind the horn but never on it; keep the horn visible and unchanged. Maintain the original scale: <<<image_1>>> is slightly larger than the cats.\nNo sash, collar, badge, clothes or accessories.\n\nSTYLE:\nPremium Japanese theatrical hand-drawn 2D cel animation. Clean lines, restrained two-tone shading, warm painted backgrounds and bright afternoon light consistent with the references. Draw actual character movement with anticipation, weight, expressive key poses and in-betweens. Short eager steps, lively ears, responsive tails and cheerful physical comedy. Keep <<<image_1>>>'s stone volume solid.\n\nEDITING:\nOne connected film scene with clear cuts between the shots below. Keep the same characters, geography, light and movement across cuts. Travel remains left to right; keep the camera on the same side of the movement axis. Inserts continue the action already established. Cut separately to the observer's reaction. A fixed camera still contains animated acting.\n\nPART ONE STAGING:\nThe opening is a brief shared nap on the hill, followed by waking up and an enthusiastic departure. Show the wake-up before any walking. <<<image_1>>> begins belly-down on the ground with all four cats from <<<image_4>>> sleeping nearby. Once awake, everyone is eager to go; the ginger from <<<image_4>>> joins the departure immediately. After the climbing action, the ginger from <<<image_4>>> remains on <<<image_1>>>'s head; only three cats from <<<image_4>>> continue on the ground. Keep this arrangement through the ending.\nUse <<<image_2>>> for the whole descent and <<<image_3>>> only for the entrance architecture at the end. Characters look toward the path, the town and one another.\n\nSHOT 1 — 00:00-00:03\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: <<<image_1>>>; all four cats from <<<image_4>>>; hillside <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: WIDE ESTABLISHING SHOT (WS).\nANGLE: Elevated rear three-quarter view.\nCOMPOSITION: <<<image_1>>> sleeps belly-down in the left foreground. The four cats from <<<image_4>>> sleep in separate nearby spots. The path descends toward the town on the right.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Gentle crane down toward the resting group.\nCHARACTER ACTION: <<<image_1>>> and all four cats from <<<image_4>>> are asleep at the opening. Breathing gently moves their bodies; grass sways around them. Near the end of the shot, the brown tabby from <<<image_4>>> opens its eyes and lifts its head beside <<<image_1>>>'s front foot.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut closer as the waking brown tabby from <<<image_4>>> reaches toward <<<image_1>>>.\n\nSHOT 2 — 00:03-00:05\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: Brown tabby from <<<image_4>>>; <<<image_1>>>'s front foot; grass <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: INSERT CLOSE-UP (CU), cat paw and stone foot.\nANGLE: Low side angle at grass level.\nCOMPOSITION: The brown tabby's forepaw from <<<image_4>>> enters from the left toward <<<image_1>>>'s resting front foot on the right. Faces remain outside the frame.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Locked-off camera.\nCHARACTER ACTION: The brown tabby from <<<image_4>>> gently taps <<<image_1>>>'s front foot twice with one small paw, inviting the guardian to get up. <<<image_1>>>'s foot gives a tiny response.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut to <<<image_1>>> waking in response to the same touch.\n\nSHOT 3 — 00:05-00:08\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: <<<image_1>>>; all four cats from <<<image_4>>>; same resting place <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: MEDIUM WIDE (MWS), <<<image_1>>>'s whole body readable.\nANGLE: Low side three-quarter angle.\nCOMPOSITION: <<<image_1>>> fills the foreground, facing right, with the brown tabby from <<<image_4>>> beside the front foot. The ginger, calico and tuxedo from <<<image_4>>> are visible farther back in their established resting places.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Small tilt up following <<<image_1>>> rising.\nCHARACTER ACTION: <<<image_1>>>'s ear twitches, eyes open toward the brown tabby from <<<image_4>>>, and the head lifts. <<<image_1>>> plants the four short feet and rises eagerly. Behind <<<image_1>>>, the ginger, calico and tuxedo from <<<image_4>>> wake and get to their feet, ears perked and ready to join. Keep the main focus on <<<image_1>>>'s wake-up reaction.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut on the ginger from <<<image_4>>> stepping toward the newly standing <<<image_1>>>.\n\nSHOT 4 — 00:08-00:13\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: <<<image_1>>>; ginger cat from <<<image_4>>>; hillside <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: FULL TWO-SHOT, both complete bodies visible.\nANGLE: Eye-level side view.\nCOMPOSITION: <<<image_1>>> faces right near the center. The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> approaches from behind on the left. The other three cats from <<<image_4>>> remain nearby outside this framing.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Short lateral move following the ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> toward <<<image_1>>>, then settle to show the climb.\nCHARACTER ACTION: <<<image_1>>> has just stood up and waits on the same level patch of grass. The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> makes one small hop onto <<<image_1>>>'s back, then climbs forward over the shoulders and settles on <<<image_1>>>'s broad head behind the horn. Show this continuous climbing route clearly. <<<image_1>>> steadies the body under the small added weight.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut after the ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> has settled securely.\n\nSHOT 5 — 00:13-00:15\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: <<<image_1>>>; ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> still seated on the head; hillside <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: MEDIUM CLOSE-UP (MCU), centered on <<<image_1>>>'s expression.\nANGLE: Eye-level side three-quarter angle.\nCOMPOSITION: <<<image_1>>>'s face and unchanged horn are readable; the ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> remains visibly perched behind the horn. The ginger cat performs no new action here.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Locked-off camera.\nCHARACTER ACTION: <<<image_1>>> glances upward toward the ginger cat from <<<image_4>>>, then raises the chin with playful official pride, accepting the new little lookout. The reaction is alert and amused.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut on <<<image_1>>> taking the first downhill step.\n\nSHOT 6 — 00:15-00:20\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: <<<image_1>>>; all four cats from <<<image_4>>>; downhill path <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: WIDE SHOT (WS), full group and landscape.\nANGLE: Low eye-level side view.\nCOMPOSITION: <<<image_1>>> leads with the ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> on the head. The brown tabby, calico and tuxedo from <<<image_4>>> trot on the ground at staggered depths.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Smooth lateral tracking to the right at their pace.\nCHARACTER ACTION: <<<image_1>>> sets off downhill with a jaunty little walk. The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> balances with its paws on <<<image_1>>>'s head, clear of the horn. The other three cats from <<<image_4>>> keep up with springy short strides and lively tails. Everyone is pleased to be going on patrol.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut closer to the mounted lookout while the walk continues.\n\nSHOT 7 — 00:20-00:23\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: Ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> on <<<image_1>>>'s head; moving hillside <<<image_2>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: MEDIUM SHOT (MS), the rider and supporting head.\nANGLE: Slightly low side three-quarter view.\nCOMPOSITION: The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> is above <<<image_1>>>'s brow; <<<image_1>>>'s horn remains visible in front of the seated cat.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Track alongside the walking <<<image_1>>> without orbiting.\nCHARACTER ACTION: The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> raises one forepaw above its eyes and leans forward slightly, pretending to be a very important lookout. The ginger cat from <<<image_4>>> makes one tiny balance adjustment to <<<image_1>>>'s walking rhythm.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut to the three companions keeping pace on the ground.\n\nSHOT 8 — 00:23-00:25\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: Brown tabby, calico and tuxedo from <<<image_4>>>; path <<<image_2>>>. <<<image_1>>> carries the ginger from <<<image_4>>> just ahead, off-screen.\nSHOT SIZE: MEDIUM WIDE (MWS), three full cat bodies.\nANGLE: Cat-eye-level side view.\nCOMPOSITION: The three ground cats from <<<image_4>>> are staggered diagonally, leaving open path on screen right.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Lateral tracking right with the three cats.\nCHARACTER ACTION: The brown tabby, calico and tuxedo from <<<image_4>>> trot eagerly downhill with individually different foot rhythms. The calico from <<<image_4>>> briefly looks up toward <<<image_1>>> and the ginger from <<<image_4>>> off-screen, then bounds forward to keep pace.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: Cut wider on the same forward movement.\n\nSHOT 9 — 00:25-00:30\nREFERENCES IN THIS SHOT: <<<image_1>>>; all four cats from <<<image_4>>>; path and sky <<<image_2>>>; village entrance architecture <<<image_3>>>.\nSHOT SIZE: WIDE SHOT (WS), the complete team within the approaching village landscape.\nANGLE: Elevated rear three-quarter angle on the same side of the travel axis.\nCOMPOSITION: <<<image_1>>> carries the ginger from <<<image_4>>>, with the other three cats from <<<image_4>>> around them. The path meets the first house's outer wall on the right.\nCAMERA MOVEMENT: Smooth following track behind and beside the team.\nCHARACTER ACTION: <<<image_1>>> and all four cats from <<<image_4>>> continue their happy descent and arrive at the village entrance. The ginger from <<<image_4>>> stays on <<<image_1>>>'s head as lookout. Keep their bodies animated with bright, eager walking rhythms.\nEDIT TO NEXT SHOT: End at the entrance while still walking. The next part begins here with the ginger from <<<image_4>>> still riding on <<<image_1>>>'s head.\n\nAUDIO:\nOnly bright outdoor ambience, breeze through grass, small stone footsteps, cat paw steps and occasional natural cat chirps. No background music, score, song, dialogue or narration.\n\nEXCLUSIONS:\nNo returning to sleep after waking, reluctance to join the patrol, melancholy, flowerpots, laundry, bicycles, rescue subplot, extra animals or people, costume changes, flying, text, subtitles, logos or watermark. No photorealism, 3D, slideshow, split-screen, puppet animation or camera-only movement.",
