      "submitterName": "장진수",
      "participantCategory": "일반인",
      "submitterAffiliation": "영상 크리에이터",
      "nationalHeritageName": "경복궁, 훈민정음(한글 창제와 자모를 모티프로 활용), 농악, 줄다리기, 광주 칠석 고싸움놀이, 강강술래",
      "baekjeRelated": "사용함",
      "description": "〈먹빛의 연희〉는 유산에 깃든 사람들의 관계에서 출발했습니다. \n사람들이 모이던 공간, 뜻을 나누는 문자, 함께 호흡을 맞추는 놀이를 한 흐름에 담고 싶었습니다. 우리의 유산을 오늘의 관객이 가까이 느끼게 하는 힘도 그 안에 살아 있는 사람들의 모습에 있다고 생각했습니다.\n\n먹이 번져 형상을 만들고, 그림 속 사람들이 움직이기 시작하는 구조를 택한 이유도 여기에 있습니다. 종이에 남은 흔적이 살아 있는 몸짓으로 바뀌는 과정에, 전해 받은 문화를 다시 행하고 나누는 전승의 의미를 담고 싶었습니다. 한지의 여백과 먹의 번짐은 서로 다른 유산을 하나의 흐름으로 연결해 줍니다. 관객의 눈앞에서 그림이 완성되는 동안, 유산을 함께 발견하는 경험이 생기기를 바랐습니다.\n\n작품의 웅장함은 함께 힘을 모으는 몸짓에서 찾았습니다. 큰 줄을 당기고 고를 들어 올리는 순간에는 수많은 사람의 호흡이 필요합니다. 손을 맞잡은 원 역시 누구 하나의 움직임만으로 완성되지 않습니다. 이러한 관계가 느껴지도록 인물들의 동작과 주변의 반응, 공간의 깊이를 살렸습니다. 시작의 작은 원형이 마지막에 사람들이 이루는 원으로 이어지는 구성에도 ‘함께할 때 완성되는 유산’이라는 생각을 담았습니다.",
      "aiTools": [
        "이미지는 ChatGPT",
        "영상은 Seedance 2.0으로 제작"
      ],
      "postEditingUsage": "사용함",
      "postEditingDetails": "Adobe Premiere Pro로 생성된 영상 클립의 선별·배열, 컷 편집, 장면 길이 조정, 배경음악 배치와 최종 영상 출력을 진행",
      "promptSummary": "",
      "fullPrompt": "[1. Opening — A Circle Emerging from Ink]\n\nCreate a 16:9 landscape video. A small circle of light gradually appears at the center of a dark ink-colored field. Its edge is soft and irregular, like wet ink spreading where a brush touches hanji paper, rather than a perfectly smooth digital shape. The camera slowly pushes toward the center, giving the small circle a growing presence. Warm ivory spreads outward from within the circle, opening a space in which the next painting can emerge. Build depth through the tonal relationship between ink and hanji, without making the circle look like a lens flare or a planet. Keep the frame free of unnecessary particles and patterns. Begin with restraint and a sense of anticipation for the expansive world about to unfold.\n\n[2. Gyeongbokgung Palace and Gwanghwamun — Entering the Painted Space]\n\nPreserve the reference image's warm hanji palette and Korean sumukhwa style with restrained color. A wet ink stroke travels sideways across blank hanji. The stone base and its three arched gateways emerge from this stroke. Columns, the roofs of the two-story gate pavilion, and the long palace walls follow in brushstrokes of varying thickness and ink density. Let forms appear where the ink actually passes, rather than revealing the entire finished image through a simple opacity fade.\n\nAs the architecture takes shape, the royal guards and flags in the forecourt begin to move. Give the scene depth through the broad movement of nearby flags, measured steps in the middle distance, and smaller movements within the rear ranks. Maintain a wide composition as the camera moves gently forward and sideways from a low viewpoint. Create natural parallax between foreground flags and Gwanghwamun so the viewer feels drawn into the location. Keep the building's structure and the characters on its signboard stable throughout the movement. Convey grandeur through scale and perspective while retaining the painted surface. Do not transition into photography or a smooth 3D render.\n\n[3. King Sejong and Hunminjeongeum — A Brushstroke Carried to Others]\n\nRender the reference image's King Sejong, scholars, palace setting, and Hangul letters in Korean sumukhwa with restrained color. A powerful dark brushstroke crosses bright, warm ivory hanji. Wooden floors, palace buildings, and the flowing hems of the figures emerge along its path. Build the forms through pressure-sensitive strokes that thicken and taper, together with the diffusion of wet ink. Do not cover the entire image with smoke.\n\nBegin with a small movement of Sejong's brush, then let the ink's flow and the Hangul letters unfold into the wider space. A scholar passes a sheet of paper, and the receiving figure responds with a natural turn of the body and gaze. The camera moves gently from left to right along the direction of the brushstroke, revealing the relationship between the person writing and those receiving the writing. Keep each Hangul character recognizable instead of breaking the letters into decorative particles. Avoid exaggerated dancing and overcrowding the frame with text. Preserve a wide composition and expressive brushwork so the act of sharing written language remains clear. No photorealism.\n\n[4. Nongak — Rhythm Moving Through Bodies and Brushstrokes]\n\nPreserve the reference image's nongak performers, instruments, and clothing. A fast, forceful diagonal ink stroke crosses warm hanji, drawing the buk drum, janggu, sogo, and the performers' full bodies. Footwork and instrument strikes begin as soon as each body takes shape, connecting the emergence of the painting naturally to the beginning of the performance.\n\nThe nearby buk player bends and straightens the knees while striking the drum. The janggu player turns the upper body to answer the rhythm. The sogo player plants a foot and turns, while the long white hat ribbon follows the head's rotation with a slight delay and a broad flowing arc. Let the performers look toward one another and respond with movements on different beats, rather than repeating identical actions. The camera tracks briefly beside them within the performance ground, then follows a gentle curve so the overlap between foreground and background figures changes. Stay far enough away to keep full bodies and instruments readable. Keep each ribbon attached to its performer's hat, never to a hand or drumstick. Ink gestures should grow from the movement of sleeves and garment hems. Maintain the painted texture throughout.\n\n[5. Traditional Tug-of-War — Many Bodies, One Shared Pull]\n\nPreserve the reference image's large straw main rope, branching side ropes, central connection, and two opposing teams. A long, thick ink stroke spreads diagonally across bright ivory hanji and becomes the main rope. Finer strokes branch outward, revealing the side ropes and the people holding them in sequence. Use lighter ink for the distant ranks and strong dark ink for nearby participants to establish the depth of the broad festival ground.\n\nThe two teams shift their weight in opposite directions and pull. They first bend their knees and brace their feet, then lean backward together for one forceful, coordinated heave. Connect the rope's tension visibly to the bodies reacting against it. Sleeves and headbands follow the movement with a slight delay. The central wooden joining pin, or binyeomok, secures the connection between the two rope sections and moves with them. It must not become a stake driven into the ground.\n\nBegin from a wide oblique viewpoint that shows both teams, then move gently along the main rope. Keep the effort of nearby participants and the long ranks receding behind them visible together. Maintain stable hand-to-rope contact, participant count, and anatomy. Create a sense of presence through the physical pull and the bodies responding to it, rather than relying on camera shake. Avoid photographic textures, unnecessary dust, and particle effects.\n\n[6. Gossaum Nori — Collective Strength Rising from the Shoulders]\n\nPreserve the reference image's two go structures, their leaders, and the relationship between the structures and the many carriers beneath them. Broad ink strokes surge upward across warm, bright hanji, sequentially drawing the ranks of people, the horizontal carrying poles on their shoulders, the long bodies of the go, and their loop-shaped heads. The carriers begin stepping and shifting their weight while the structures are still emerging. Do not reduce the sequence to a zoom into a finished painting.\n\nBoth teams lower their knees, take short steps forward, and then lift their go in coordination. Each leader balances through the knees and torso while signaling from atop the structure. The two looped heads meet at the center and push upward against one another. At contact, show the weight transferring through shoulders, arms, torsos, and braced feet. The go must not float independently or rest on support posts fixed to the ground.\n\nApproach from a wide oblique viewpoint at crowd level. As the two go rise, tilt the camera upward to follow them and arc gently to one side. Nearby carriers, the two structures in the middle distance, and spectators behind them should move across the frame at different rates, creating natural spatial depth. Keep the loops, leaders, and shoulder-bearing teams readable within the same frame. Flags, flowing garments, and the spectators' responses add the energy of a communal festival. Preserve forceful Korean ink-and-color brushwork throughout, with no melting structures or figures merging into the timber.\n\n[7. Ganggangsullae — Closing with a Circle Made Together]\n\nPreserve the reference image's women holding hands, full moon, shoreline, and pine tree as a Korean ink-and-color painting on warm hanji. A gently curving ink stroke traces a circle and flows into the dancers' skirt hems, while lighter ink washes reveal the surrounding landscape. Let clothing, bodies, and linked hands emerge along the brush's path instead of bringing the entire image into focus at once.\n\nThe circular formation moves in one direction with coordinated steps. Bodies rise and fall gently with the footwork, while joined hands and arms respond fluidly to the movement of neighboring dancers. Skirts trail the rotation slightly, making each person's motion feel connected to the next. The camera moves gently around the outside of the circle, then pulls back a little to end on a wide view of both dancers and landscape. Maintain the hand connections and the number of dancers. Keep the moon and pine anchored within the scene rather than moving with the camera. Let the force of the preceding sequence settle into warmth and a lingering sense of connection. Preserve the tonal richness of the ink and the luminous open space of the hanji to the end. Do not transition into photorealism or the surface appearance of 3D animation.",
