tural daylight, refined museum documentary style, true-to-life color, realistic ceramic and glaze texture, clean and elegant composition, subtle poetic mood, shallow depth of field, crisp material detail, no face, no dramatic cinematic lighting, no dark moody tone, no text --ar 16:9 --stylize 85 --v 7\n\n\n[Midjourney - 가마 소성]\n\nthe same Korean ceramic vessel inside a traditional kiln during firing, warm kiln fire surrounding the vessel, realistic heat shimmer, the pale glazed surface gradually developing into a translucent celadon finish through the firing process, believable material transformation, the same vessel design throughout the sequence, part of a continuous Korean celadon-making process, realistic kiln interior, controlled warm firelight, refined museum documentary style, true-to-life ceramic texture, clean and elegant composition, subtle poetic mood, detailed surface quality, no people visible, no exaggerated flames, no magical transformation, no fantasy effects, no dramatic cinematic lighting, no dark moody tone, no text --ar 16:9 --stylize 90 --v 7\n\n\n[Midjourney - 완성된 비색 청자]\n\nextreme macro close-up of the same finished Korean celadon vessel under bright natural daylight, the glaze revealing subtle layered tones of celadon blue-green, pale gray-blue, soft jade, and muted turquoise, realistic translucent glaze depth, delicate natural reflections moving across the elegant curved ceramic surface, the same vessel design throughout the sequence, part of a continuous Korean celadon-making process, refined museum artifact photography, true-to-life celadon color, realistic ceramic texture, clean and elegant composition, subtle poetic mood, crisp fine detail, no heavy color grading, no dramatic cinematic lighting, no dark moody tone, no text --ar 16:9 --stylize 75 --v 7\n\n\n[Midjourney - 비색의 디지털 공간]\n\na refined final composition connecting a clear Korean autumn sky, an authentic Korean celadon vessel, and a subtle digital celadon color environment, bright clean natural daylight, the sky blue softly reflected on the glaze, elegant visual continuity from nature to ceramic to digital color, museum exhibition film aesthetic, true-to-life celadon color, serene and sophisticated, minimal architectural composition, premium but realistic, no dramatic shadows, no fantasy glow, no neon, no cyberpunk, no text --ar 16:9 --stylize 110 --v 7\n\n\n[Kling Omni - 이미지 전환]\n\nUse the uploaded images as fixed visual keyframes and connect them in the exact order provided.\n\nPreserve each image faithfully.\nDo not redesign the celadon vase, architecture, colors, shadows, lighting, or composition.\nDo not add people, hands, props, text, or extra objects.\n\nCreate a premium minimalist transition film using slow camera movement, gentle parallax, architectural match cuts, color-plane transitions, and light and shadow sweeps.\n\nThe transitions should feel elegant and impactful, like a high-end art commercial or exhibition teaser.\n\nUse slow push-ins, subtle side glides, geometric wipes, panel-based transitions, shadow sweeps, composition match cuts, and refined light transitions.\n\nPreserve the celadon vase exactly.\nPreserve the minimalist architectural mood.\nPreserve the celadon, sky-blue, and off-white palette.\nPreserve the clean lighting and elegant shadows.\n\nAvoid morphing, melting, surreal transformation, changing vase shape, changing colors, adding objects, flashy effects, fast camera spins, excessive zooms, neon tones, sci-fi effects, text, subtitles, and watermarks.\n\nThe result should feel clean, refined, minimal, calm, architectural, and visually striking, like a premium art-film or exhibition teaser.\n\n\n[Kling Omni - 청자 제작 과정]\n\nCreate a realistic multi-shot video showing the traditional Korean celadon-making process.\n\nKeep the same ceramic vessel throughout the sequence.\n\nShow the process in this exact order:\n1. shaping wet clay on a potter’s wheel\n2. forming and refining the ceramic shape\n3. first firing\n4. applying translucent celadon glaze\n5. final firing\n6. revealing the completed celadon vessel\n\nUse bright natural daylight for workshop scenes and warm realistic kiln light for firing scenes.\n\nKeep the ceramic shape, proportions, and material appearance consistent throughout.\n\nShow clear physical transformation between each stage.\n\nThe transformation must look physically believable and based on traditional ceramic production, not magical.\n\nRefined museum-documentary tone, true-to-life ceramic texture, realistic material behavior, clean and elegant visual quality.\n\nAvoid dark cinematic grading, exaggerated flames, fantasy effects, sci-fi elements, text, subtitles, and watermarks.\n\n\n[Kling / Grok - 비색의 디지털 기록]\n\nStart with the celadon vase in a bright, quiet museum-like environment.\n\nRather than creating obvious color bands or graphic strips, extract a few subtle color tones from different areas of the glaze and present them as refined, minimal color samples.\n\nThese samples should appear as elegant translucent planes, soft material swatches, or restrained floating panels arranged in a clean architectural composition around the vase.\n\nThe tones should include pale blue-green, muted jade, gray-blue, soft turquoise, and other subtle celadon variations, but they must feel curated and sophisticated rather than decorative or graphic.\n\nKeep the physical celadon vase visible and central, so the viewer understands that the palette comes directly from the glaze surface.\n\nThe motion should be quiet, precise, and premium, like a museum archive or luxury exhibition film.\n\nDo not create rainbow-like bars, infographic-style color strips, flashy digital graphics, or decorative gradients.\n\nDo not make the color extraction feel magical.\n\nThe result should feel like elegant preservation and refined visual analysis.\n\nBright clean museum lighting, minimal architectural background, restrained composition, high-end art direction, no neon, no cyberpunk, no sci-fi interface, no text.\n\n\n[Suno AI - 배경음악]\n\nKorean traditional instrumental, 60-second gradual build: spacious, delicate Geomungo and Gayageum plucks open; aching Ajaeng and breathy Daegeum enter as the mood deepens into Han; Buk and Janggu drive a continuously rising, majestic final 10 seconds to a decisive stop, Pure acoustic traditional textures",
