      "fullPrompt": "IMAGE GENERATION\n\nS01_Mask_Start - Opening Mask Close-Up\nCreate one cinematic 16:9 landscape reference image for the opening mask close-up of “Five Directions, One Rhythm,” a contemporary artistic interpretation inspired by Cheoyongmu. Use the approved yellow BC1 dancer as the identity, costume and material reference, and rephotograph this same miniature character at close range without redesigning it. Compose a nearly frontal, centred close-up of the rigid red painted mask with the upper chest visible below it. Include both eyes, the complete nose, fixed smiling mouth, teeth, curled moustache, full pointed black goatee and both circular earrings. Upper headdress ornaments may extend beyond the top edge, but keep the entire goatee inside the frame. Exclude hands, arms, feet and other dancers. Match the broad red face, sculpted black eyebrows, painted eyes, broad nose, white teeth, black moustache and beard exactly. The face is a physical painted mask with a fixed expression, not living skin or makeup. Retain the visible black headdress, peach and flower ornaments, silver-grey earrings, grey bead necklace, ivory collar, mustard-yellow robe and deep teal shoulder and chest panels. Hold the head upright and almost directly toward the camera, with relaxed shoulders and no dramatic tilt. Use a softly mottled midnight-indigo background without visible mountains or a sun. Warm amber light from screen-right shapes the mask, with gentle fill preserving detail on the opposite side. Maintain tactile fabric-miniature realism, detailed woven cloth, controlled painted-surface highlights and sharp facial markings. No text, borders, additional faces, prism reflections, trails, glow, fog, particles, deformation or motion effects.\n\nS02_Lead_Gathered_Start - Yellow Lead and Ensemble\nCreate one cinematic 16:9 starting-frame image using the approved BC1 five-dancer ensemble as the authority for identities, costumes, formation and stage. Place the yellow dancer prominently at front centre, with white at front screen-left, red at front screen-right, black behind and left of centre, and blue behind and right of centre. All five face the audience, with the yellow dancer’s rounded forearms gathered inward below chest level and the other dancers in compatible low gathered poses. Keep torsos upright, knees softly flexed and feet naturally grounded. Preserve exactly two attached cream-white sleeve extensions per dancer, fully concealing hands and fingers. Keep the yellow dancer’s central costume panels readable between the weighted sleeve folds. Preserve every mask, ornament, bead necklace, embroidered pattern, belt and robe colour. Frame all five dancers with room for a small lateral dance phrase, showing every shoe and maintaining clear separation. Use the approved indigo paper theatre, restrained amber sun disc, unchanged floor seams and warm amber lighting against dark midnight-indigo surroundings. No text, additional dancers, new props, visible fingers, detached scarves, exaggerated motion, fog, trails or reflections.\n\nS04_Ensemble_Start - Frontal Five-Dancer Ensemble\nCreate one cinematic 16:9 landscape starting-frame image of the approved BC1 five-dancer ensemble, a contemporary artistic interpretation inspired by Cheoyongmu. Use the approved ensemble image as the authority for character identities, costume designs, stage geometry, materials and formation. Include exactly five dancers: white at front screen-left, black behind and slightly left of centre, yellow at front centre, blue behind and slightly right of centre, and red at front screen-right. Maintain clear separation and show all five masks, headdresses and pairs of shoes. Preserve each robe colour, cream botanical embroidery, teal chest and cuff details, red front panels, belts, tassels, grey bead necklaces, earrings and black ornamented headdresses. Match the rigid red painted masks, fixed eyes and teeth, black eyebrows, moustaches and goatees. Each dancer has exactly two cream-white sleeve extensions attached at the cuffs, fully concealing hands and fingers. All five face the audience in balanced low-open stances with softly flexed knees, upright torsos, relaxed shoulders and gently rounded elbows. Forearms extend diagonally outward below chest level, while sleeve cloth hangs in broad weighted folds. Use a frontal audience viewpoint around waist-to-chest height, with modest headroom, side clearance and floor beneath every shoe. Preserve the layered indigo mountains, restrained amber sun disc, theatre wings, floor texture and seams. Use warm amber illumination, gentle fill and grounded shadows within deep midnight-indigo surroundings. No text, extra limbs, visible fingers, detached sleeves, overlapping faces, prism effects, trails, fog, particles or motion blur.\n\n\nL03_Climax_Gathered_Start - Ensemble Climax Preparation\nCreate one cinematic 16:9 starting image of the approved BC1 ensemble preparing for a coordinated upward-and-outward sleeve phrase. Preserve the five-dancer formation and each costume identity. All performers begin with softly flexed knees, upright torsos and rounded forearms gathered inward below chest level, leaving the masks and central panels readable. The cream-white sleeve extensions remain attached at both cuffs, hanging in weighted folds and fully concealing the hands. Keep every shoe grounded and every mask, headdress, bead necklace, earring and embroidered panel consistent with the approved designs. Use a slightly tighter frontal composition with room above and beside the dancers for the later sleeve opening. Preserve the indigo paper stage, amber sun disc, floor and warm amber lighting. No raised starting sleeves, airborne cloth, additional dancers, visible fingers, text, fog, reflections, trails or motion blur.\n\nVIDEO GENERATION \n\nS01 - Reference: S01_Mask_Start\nCreate one continuous 6-second cinematic close-up from the uploaded S01 mask image. Preserve the exact rigid painted mask, eye shapes, pupils, eyebrows, nose, fixed mouth, teeth, moustache, pointed goatee, earrings, headdress and visible costume. From 0.0-0.4 seconds, hold the nearly frontal upright starting pose. From 0.4-2.1 seconds, slowly tilt the entire head approximately 2-3 degrees toward screen-left, keeping the face almost directly toward the camera. From 2.1-4.5 seconds, move smoothly through upright into an equally small 2-3-degree tilt toward screen-right. From 4.5-5.7 seconds, return gradually to upright, then settle from 5.7-6.0 seconds. Create restrained curiosity and mystery through the small deliberate movement, without an exaggerated horror pose. Keep the torso almost still and the camera locked. Painted eyes and expression remain unchanged throughout; earrings and beads may respond minimally without changing shape. Preserve warm amber light, the midnight-indigo background and consistent exposure. No side-facing head turn, blinking, eye movement, mouth animation, facial morphing, camera shake, reflections, trails, text or audio.\n\nS02 - Reference: S02_Lead_Gathered_Start\nCreate one continuous 8-second cinematic ensemble shot from the uploaded image. Preserve the yellow lead and all four surrounding dancers, their exact costumes, rigid masks and stage positions. From 0.0-0.5 seconds, establish the gathered starting pose. From 0.5-1.8 seconds, the yellow lead makes a shallow knee-led pulse and smoothly opens the rounded forearms. From 1.8-3.5 seconds, the lead travels a short distance toward screen-right using small grounded steps while the camera tracks smoothly in the same direction, keeping the lead prominent. From 3.5-4.1 seconds, ease the lateral travel and gather the forearms slightly to prepare the return. From 4.1-6.5 seconds, the same dancer steps toward screen-left while the camera follows, returning the lead to the original central stage position. From 6.5-8.0 seconds, settle into a balanced low-open pose. The other dancers remain in their established areas and perform restrained compatible pulses and sleeve gestures. Keep hands concealed, sleeve cloth attached and masks fixed. Maintain coherent floor perspective, stationary scenery, warm amber lighting and dark indigo surroundings. No abrupt reversal, foot sliding, spins, cast substitution, background morphing, trails, text or audio.\n\nS04 - Reference: S04_Ensemble_Start\nCreate one continuous 4-second ensemble shot preserving the uploaded five-dancer formation and all costume identities. From 0.0-2.0 seconds, all five dancers softly bend their knees while rounded forearms gather inward below chest level, with the white dancer beginning only slightly ahead of the others to give the phrase a subtle lead. Keep torsos upright and masks clearly readable. From 2.0-4.0 seconds, complete the gather and allow the attached sleeve folds to settle with natural weight. Use one restrained diagonal camera slide toward audience-right without crossing through the performers or changing their stage positions. Maintain clear separation between the five masks, concealed hands, fixed facial paint, stationary scenery and coherent floor perspective. Preserve warm amber lighting and midnight-indigo surroundings. No strong stagger, independent freestyle gestures, extra performers, sleeve detachment, morphing, trails, text or audio.\n\n\nL03 - Reference: L03_Climax_Gathered_Start\nCreate one continuous 8-second cinematic ensemble climax from the uploaded gathered starting pose. From 0.0-1.0 seconds, perform a small shared gathering pulse. From 1.0-2.0 seconds, deepen the knee bend slightly with a restrained coordinated shoulder orientation toward screen-right, keeping torsos controlled. From 2.0-3.2 seconds, rise strongly and cast both rounded forearms upward and outward in one clear energetic phrase, allowing the attached cream-white sleeves to expand into broad weighted arcs without becoming airborne ribbons. From 3.2-5.5 seconds, let the sleeve cloth descend naturally as the arms return toward a dignified low-open position. From 5.5-8.0 seconds, settle into balanced ensemble stillness with subtle breathing-like weight adjustments. Use one smooth camera arc of approximately 10 degrees combined with a modest push toward the group. Preserve all five identities, fixed painted masks, headdresses, jewellery and concealed hands. Keep the scenery stationary and floor perspective coherent. No jump, repeated sleeve cast, full spin, body collision, cloth detachment, morphing, trails, text or audio.\n\nSOUNDTRACK GENERATION - MUREKA\nCinematic traditional Korean court and mask dance soundtrack (Gugak hybrid). High-energy, blood-pumping, dramatic score driven by traditional Korean instrumentation, punchy sub-bass, and dynamic ritual percussion. [Instruments] Drums: Janggu (hourglass drum syncopated rolls), Buk (barrel drum heavy bass hits). Melodic: Daegeum (large transverse bamboo flute), piercing Piri (double-reed pipe), expressive Haegeum (two-string bowed fiddle). Foley/SFX: Wooden mask movement rustles, creaking friction, metallic resonance. [Structure] Dark intro with low atmospheric sub-bass drone, eerie suspense, and wooden mask movement sound effects. Deepens into a mysterious, ominous mood with breathy Daegeum bends and tense Haegeum glissandos. Choreography Sync: Sudden burst into fast, blood-pumping rhythm! Heavy Buk bass strikes and intense Janggu driving beats sync precisely with energetic dance movements and rapid video cuts. Exciting, high-intensity.",
