      "nationalHeritageName": "공주 고마나루(명승) — 고마나루 곰 설화, 곰사당의 돌곰, 금강과 솔숲을 모티프로 활용",
      "baekjeRelated": "사용함",
      "description": "[고마나루, 다시 흐르는 밤]\n떠나간 사람을 기다리던 곰 여인이, 자신을 기억하는 도시를 만난다.\n\n작품 내용 :\n\n달빛이 스며든 곰사당에서 돌곰이 천천히 깨어난다. 거친 돌 표면에 흔들리던 물빛은 강물 속 여인의 모습으로 이어지고, 물안개 사이로 곰 여인이 나타난다. 곰굴에 남은 작은 가족의 그림자와 멀어지는 나룻배는 그녀의 상실과 기다림을 보여준다.\n\n여인의 손끝에서 떨어진 물방울은 파문을 일으키며 시간을 오늘의 금강으로 연결한다. 고마나루 솔숲을 지나온 여인은 황포돛배와 유등, 축제를 함께 즐기는 사람들을 마주한다. 불빛이 그녀의 눈에 스며들고, 밤은 새벽의 곰사당과 강, 솔숲으로 이어진다.\n\n창작의도 :\n이 작품은 공주 고마나루 설화에 담긴 상실과 기다림을 ‘기억과 위안’의 이야기로 확장하고자 기획하였다. 오랜 시간 돌의 형상으로 남아 있던 곰 여인이 오늘의 도시를 바라보는 상상을 통해, 유산이 현재의 삶과 만나는 순간을 표현한다.\n\n‘돌·물·빛’을 주요 시각 언어로 사용하였다. 돌은 오랜 기억을, 강물과 파문은 시간의 흐름을, 도시와 축제의 빛은 사람들의 온기와 이어지는 삶을 상징한다. 생성형 AI로 석상의 변신과 수면의 반영, 과거에서 현재로 이어지는 장면을 구현하여 눈에 보이지 않는 설화의 기억을 영화적 이미지로 풀어냈다.\n\n곰 여인이 현대 축제를 만나는 장면은 설화와 오늘의 금강 풍경을 연결한 창작적 해석이다. 축제의 불빛을 기억과 위안의 상징으로 삼아, 오래된 이야기가 오늘의 사람들에게 다시 전해지는 모습을 담았다. 마지막 새벽 풍경에는 고마나루의 강과 솔숲에 남은 기억을 다음 세대로 이어가고자 하는 마음을 담았다.",
      "aiTools": [
        "OpenAI 이미지 생성 도구(이미지 제작)",
        "Higgsfield AI(이미지 기반 영상 생성)"
      ],
      "postEditingUsage": "사용함",
      "postEditingDetails": "CapCut(컷 편집, 장면 연결, 자막 편집)",
      "promptSummary": "",
      "fullPrompt": "곰 여인 : Tool: built-in image_gen\nReference 1: primary-face-reference.png (exclusive identity)\nReference 2: ../04-earlier-concepts-and-prompts/yeoul-baekje-costume-01.png (costume, hairstyle and layout only)\n\nCreate a photorealistic six-panel costume and character continuity sheet for an adult Korean bear-woman character in a cinematic retelling of the Gomanaru legend, set in the Baekje period. REFERENCE 1 is the exclusive facial identity reference: preserve her gently hooded dark almond eyes, natural straight eyebrows, softly broad nose tip, full slightly parted pink lips, rounded jaw, subtle freckles, rosy cheeks and realistic skin pores. Keep her unmistakably the same adult woman across all views. REFERENCE 2 is ONLY a reference for costume, hairstyle structure, panel arrangement, and studio lighting. Do NOT copy or blend the face from reference 2.\n\nMatch the six-panel arrangement in reference 2: three columns and two rows, top row front full-length, three-quarter full-length, back full-length; bottom row front head-and-shoulders, true side-profile head-and-shoulders, rear hairstyle closeup. Consistent scale within each row, complete feet visible in full-length panels, generous clean spacing. Pale warm-gray studio background, soft natural diffuse lighting, high-detail cinematic photographic realism, natural anatomy, no text or labels.\n\nApply the historically informed but interpretive Baekje costume from reference 2: warm unbleached ivory textured plant-fiber wrap robe extending below the knees, diagonal overlapping collar with narrow muted gray-brown edging, moderately wide sleeves with understated edged cuffs, a simple thin gray-brown fabric belt at the natural waist, ankle-length plain gray-brown skirt underneath, simple dark cloth shoes. Modest, lived-in, beautiful understated textiles, no elaborate court finery, no modern accessories, no late Joseon short high-waisted jeogori, no fantasy armor.\n\nHair: black hair, center part, two braids coiled closely along the sides and back of the head as in reference 2, low compact arrangement rather than a tall bun. IMPORTANT USER CHANGE: add delicate, natural, slightly irregular wispy baby hairs along the forehead hairline, temples, in front of the ears, and at the nape. Several soft flyaway strands catch the light and gently frame her cheeks, while the main braided arrangement remains clearly readable and consistent in all angles. No crown, no ornate hairpins. Subtle neutral expression, quiet tenderness, lifelike unretouched skin. This is a creative character design informed by fragmentary historical evidence, not a claim of exact archaeological reconstruction.\n\n영상 : Keep the camera fixed. Keep the stone bear and the entire shrine completely still.\nIn the pine forest behind the shrine, a few small green-gold fireflies drift slowly and blink softly at different distances. Keep them sparse and only in the background. The thin clouds around the moon move very slightly, almost too slowly to notice.\nKeep the same dark pre-dawn lighting, soft moon, forest, shrine structure, stone-bear appearance and composition throughout the shot. Keep the light and brightness stable.\nAvoid camera movement, zoom, camera shake, moving bear, head movement, moving paws, blinking eyes, mouth movement, glowing eyes, strong wind, fast clouds, excessive fireflies, bright light changes, heavy fog, text and watermarks.\n\nFrom 0 to 1 second, the stone bear stays completely still while the camera begins a very slow and smooth move closer to its face.\n\nFrom 1 to 3 seconds, the stone bear slowly raises its head only a little toward the faint moonlight. The movement stops at a calm, natural angle. Its muzzle remains almost level and points only slightly upward. The bear must not tilt its head far back or look straight up at the sky. Only its head and neck move. Its body, front paws and pedestal stay completely still. The movement is slow, heavy and natural for an ancient stone statue.\n\nFrom 3 to 4 seconds, the bear completely stops moving and quietly holds the final head position for one full second while the camera finishes its gentle move closer.\n\nIn the pine forest behind the bear, a few small green-gold fireflies gently blink at different distances. Some are tiny and far away, and one or two are slightly closer. They move slowly and never pass in front of the bear’s face. A small amount of dust floats in the soft moonlight, with a thin mist among the pine trees.\n\nKeep the moonlight soft and dim. Keep the stone bear’s appearance, shrine, forest, lighting and composition unchanged.\n\nAvoid raising the head too high, pointing the nose toward the sky, stretching the neck, changing the face, moving the body or paws, camera shake, fast camera movement, glowing eyes, blinking eyes, mouth movement, strong moonlight, heavy fog, excessive fireflies.\n\nSTART FRAME: use the overhead image where the stone bear’s paw has just touched the river and clear circular ripples surround it.\nEND FRAME: use the revised overhead image where the water is calm, the woman’s reflection angles from the left toward the lower-right, and the moon reflection is faint.\nA single continuous 5-second shot. Keep the camera completely still in a perfectly vertical overhead view.\nFrom 0 to 1 second, the heavy stone paw completes one slow step into the shallow river. It creates a small natural splash and clear circular ripples. The paw then stops completely.\nFrom 1 to 3 seconds, the ripples spread outward and gradually weaken. The stone paw remains fixed. Thin mist and reflected clouds drift very slowly across the dark blue-gray water. The reflected moon in the upper-right stays dim, soft and slightly blurred.\nFrom 3 to 5 seconds, the water becomes calm and mirror-like. As the last ripples fade, the reflection of the same adult woman gradually becomes visible across the water. Her reflected clothing begins on the left and her reflected face appears in the lower-right. The reflection has center-parted braided-up black hair, an ivory cross-collar robe with gray-brown trim and a calm expression. It appears only because the water is becoming clear; do not show a glowing transformation effect.\nAt the end, hold the calm water and completed reflection for a brief moment. The physical woman remains outside the camera view. Preserve the exact stone paw, river texture, submerged stones, lighting and composition of the two reference frames.\nA single continuous 6-second live-action cinematic shot at a misty riverbank at dawn, beginning exactly from the supplied departing-boat image.\n0–1 second: The camera holds the same wide composition. The lone man continues rowing the small wooden boat slowly away from the riverbank. The boat moves farther into the mist and leaves a gentle V-shaped wake. The morning mist drifts softly above the calm water.\n1–4 seconds: The same young woman from the character reference runs into the frame from the left foreground, seen only from behind. She runs diagonally along the wet stones toward the water and the departing boat. Her movement is urgent but physically natural. Her arms move close to her body, her steps remain balanced, and her feet make only small splashes near the shoreline. Her long ivory robe and gray-brown skirt move with realistic weight. Her braided hair remains firmly wrapped low around the sides and back of her head. The boat continues moving away throughout her run and never turns back.\n4–5 seconds: The woman reaches the water’s edge and slows to a natural stop. She does not enter deep water. Her final step settles on the wet stones, her robe hem falls back into place, and her arms gradually lower beside her body. She remains facing the boat.\n5–6 seconds: She stands completely still from behind, staring at the distant boat in stunned silence. Her shoulders lower slightly and her posture loses strength, expressing shock, helplessness, and grief without exaggerated acting. The boat becomes slightly smaller in the distance while the water and mist continue moving gently. Hold this final moment.\nPreserve the same river, shoreline, pine-covered mountains, mist, dawn sky, sun position, boat, man, camera angle, lighting, and color throughout. Keep the camera almost fixed, with only an extremely subtle slow move forward. Maintain realistic body proportions, cloth movement, water movement, and natural depth. No dialogue and no lip movement.\nAvoid: cuts, angle changes, sudden zooms, camera orbiting, camera shake, slow-motion running, floating feet, sliding feet, unnatural leg movement, exaggerated arm waving, falling, kneeling, jumping into the river, deep water, giant woman, visible front face, hairstyle changes, costume changes, extra people, duplicate boats, boat approaching the shore, modern objects, magical effects, text, subtitles, and logos.",
