      "fullPrompt": "《왕비의 마지막 꿈》 사용 프롬프트 전문\n\nS9는 S1 화면을 재사용한 정지 장면으로, 별도 ��성 프롬��트가 없���니다.\n\nS1 이미지 — GPT Image\nA solemn painterly portrait of the wooden funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen, Baekje, Korea, reimagined as a lyrical East Asian fine-art painting.\n\nShow one complete wooden funerary headrest centered in the frame, viewed from a slightly elevated three-quarter angle. Preserve its broad upper body, narrower base, and deep semicircular cradle for the head. At both upper ends, include two small attached carved wooden phoenix-head ornaments, resting symmetrically on the top corners. These are fixed decorative parts of the artifact, not living birds.\n\nRender the object in aged cinnabar-red painted wood with fine gold hexagonal compartments and delicate floral ornamentation across the surface. The headrest should feel historical, worn, and quiet, with visible age and pigment texture. Keep the full object clearly readable and unobstructed.\n\nPlace the artifact in the lower-middle of a wide 16:9 composition, occupying roughly half the frame width. Surround it with a dark, restrained ink-black and warm brown background, with generous empty space above. Set it on a dim, quiet surface with subtle painterly shadow.\n\nStyle: expressive East Asian brushwork, visible pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, worn ochre gold, soft diffuse illumination, restrained highlights, no magical glow. The mood is still, intimate, elegiac, and contemplative, as if the surviving object carries the memory of an absent person.\n\nPainterly fine art, Korean heritage atmosphere, 16:9 landscape, no text, no people.\n\nS1 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nLocked camera. Keep the entire painted wooden headrest, its two attached phoenix-head ornaments, and every surface motif unchanged. Only a faint peripheral ink haze drifts almost imperceptibly. Preserve the existing muted highlights, worn pigment texture, framing, and dark background. No artifact movement, no emerging creatures, no speech, no music.\n\nS2 이미지 — GPT Image\nA dreamlike painted vision of entering the surface decoration of the wooden funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen.\n\nUse the corrected S1 image as the exact visual reference for the artifact’s shape, angle, color, and decorative language. Preserve the same cinnabar-red painted wood, worn gold hexagonal patterning, and attached wooden phoenix-head ornaments. The headrest itself is no longer shown whole; instead the viewer has moved into its painted surface.\n\nTransform the gold hexagonal compartments and red painted lacquer fields into a deep inward-turning painted passage. The surface should feel as if it curls open from within, becoming a swirling interior made of red-brown pigment, worn ochre-gold fragments, ink-black depth, and soft cloudlike brush haze. The central pull should remain gentle and lyrical, not like a sci-fi portal.\n\nKeep the composition spacious and readable, with the viewer looking into a single inward-flowing tunnel of pattern and pigment. The mood is hushed, mysterious, and ceremonial, as if entering a painted memory hidden inside the object.\n\nExpressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, restrained ochre gold, painterly fine art, 16:9 landscape, no text.\n\nS2 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nOne continuous, very slow forward camera move toward the existing flower-shaped center of the supplied painting. Keep the image orientation upright and steady. The surrounding crimson and gold painted layers reveal a small amount of depth as the center draws closer, with the central flower remaining recognizable. Preserve the existing curled composition without rotating it. Motion is smooth and unhurried, as though the viewer is approaching the surface of a deep painting. Retain rough brush edges, worn gold pigment, muted red and ink-black tones. No camera roll, rapid tunnel rush, flashing light, new objects, text, speech or music.\n\nS3 이미지 — GPT Image\nA dreamlike painted lotus-world unfolding inside the imagined inner space of the funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen.\n\nUse the established visual language from S1 and S2: expressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, and worn ochre-gold. The scene should feel like a continuation of the same painted film.\n\nAt the center, a great lotus blooms open from swirling red-brown and black painterly currents. Within the lotus, a softly illuminated female-like celestial presence begins to emerge. She is not the queen, but an imagined guide born from the lotus itself. Her form should remain partially painterly and delicate, not fully solid or photoreal. Let petals, gold dust, and flowing brush lines radiate outward from the center.\n\nSurround the lotus with spacious dark negative space and gently circulating pigment currents. The image should feel sacred, lyrical, and intimate rather than spectacular.\n\nPainterly fine art, expressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, restrained ochre gold, 16:9 landscape, no text.\n\nS3 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nOne continuous shot with a nearly locked camera. Begin with the lotus already open and the celestial woman already visible, exactly as in the supplied painting. The outer painted petals gently ease a little farther apart while the existing ribbon-like robe edges float softly upward. Keep the woman's head, facial features, hair ornament and body pose stable. The warm light at the lotus center remains soft and steady. Preserve the original painterly anatomy, petal shapes, aged pigment and crimson, cream, black and ochre-gold colors. No additional figure, face turn, body transformation, text, speech or music.\n\nS4 이미지 — GPT Image\nA dreamlike painted vision of the celestial guide fully revealed within the imagined afterlife world inspired by the funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen.\n\nMatch the same painted film style as S1–S3. Show the female celestial guide rising gracefully from the lotus world, her robes and ribbons flowing in long elegant arcs through the dark ink-washed space. She is not a realistic human portrait; her form is shaped through brushwork, aged pigment, and soft glowing paint. Her face remains gentle and painterly.\n\nShe extends one arm as if opening the way forward. Around her, two smaller attendant-like celestial traces may remain faint and secondary, keeping the main guide as the focal presence. The surrounding brush currents should feel like a path forming out of crimson, brown, black, and ochre-gold pigment.\n\nThe mood is solemn and guiding, as if she is leading the viewer deeper into a painted afterlife path.\n\nExpressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, restrained ochre gold, painterly fine art, 16:9 landscape, no text.\n\nS4 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nOne continuous shot with a locked camera. The celestial guide holds her existing raised-arm pose throughout. Her face, hands, hair ornament and torso remain still, and the smaller background figures retain their existing positions and number. Animate only the loose ends of the long painted robe ribbons: they drift slowly toward the upper right along their original curves, suggesting a gentle path through the painting. Preserve all visible brush textures and the muted crimson, cream, ink-black and worn gold palette. Keep the lighting steady. No finger movement, new gesture, face turn, additional person, text, speech or music.\n\nS5 이미지 — GPT Image\nA dreamlike painted vision rising above the wooden funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen.\n\nUse the corrected S1 image as the exact artifact reference. Preserve the same headrest silhouette, deep semicircular cradle, two attached wooden phoenix-head ornaments, cinnabar-red surface, and gold hexagonal decoration. Keep the entire artifact clearly visible in the lower-middle foreground, with the same viewing angle as S1.\n\nAbove the headrest are exactly two large, weightless phoenix forms made of muted crimson brushwork and worn ochre-gold pigment. Their wings are partially open in complementary upward curves, with clearly separated silhouettes and long, flowing painted tails. Fine pigment trails visually connect their tails to the headrest’s surface decoration.\n\nThe two small wooden ornaments remain attached and unchanged. The two larger phoenixes are imagined extensions of the painted motifs, not realistic animals or enlarged wooden carvings.\n\nLeave open dark space around the wings and above their heads for later animation. Keep the headrest unobstructed. No queen, no celestial woman, no additional birds.\n\nExpressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment, ink-wash atmosphere, warm brown, muted crimson, ink black, antique gold. Match S1 and the existing scene references as one continuous painted film. Quiet wonder rather than spectacle, 16:9 landscape, no text.\n\nS5 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nOne continuous shot with a locked camera. Animate the two phoenixes already present in the supplied painting. Both glide a short distance upward within the frame. The larger phoenix makes one small, slow wing flex, while the second keeps a steadier wing shape. Their long painted feather tips trail gently along the existing curves. The pillow below stays completely still with its current shape and patterns intact. Keep exactly two phoenixes and preserve their distinctive painted silhouettes, rough pigment and muted red, black and worn ochre-gold colors. No repeated flapping, crossing or merging birds, extra wings, text, speech or music.\n\nS6 이미지 — GPT Image\nA dreamlike painted vision of a fish-dragon emerging from cloud-water currents inspired by the funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen.\n\nMatch the same continuous painted film style established in S1–S5: expressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, and restrained ochre gold.\n\nShow a single fish-dragon suggested through flowing brushstrokes rather than hard anatomy. Its body should feel formed from the same cinnabar-red and gold decorative language seen on the headrest, with parts of the pattern dissolving into cloud, water, and mist. It rises diagonally through low curling cloud-water ribbons, as if crossing the boundary between water and sky.\n\nThis is not a violent dragon scene and not a fully realistic beast. The form should remain lyrical, painterly, and slightly incomplete, as if the creature is only briefly appearing within the currents. Leave generous dark negative space around the main body so the composition remains calm and readable.\n\nPainterly fine art, expressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, restrained ochre gold, 16:9 landscape, no text.\n\nS6 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nOne continuous shot with a locked camera. The single painted fish-dragon glides a short distance toward the upper right, keeping the original curve of its body, head profile and long whiskers recognizable. The existing cream-colored wave strokes drift in the same direction and gradually soften into light mist near the upper edge. Keep the body coherent and made of the same painted pigment throughout. Preserve the reference's rough brushwork, restrained crimson, ink-black and worn gold colors. No head turn, mouth opening, added limbs, duplicated creature, glossy scales, text, speech or music.\n\nS7 이미지 — GPT Image\nA final dreamlike painted encounter within the imagined afterlife world inspired by the funerary headrest of King Muryeong’s queen.\n\nMatch the same painted film established in S1–S6. Show one small rear-facing queen figure standing before a softly illuminated lotus. Her robe is muted crimson with flowing painterly edges; her face is unseen. Preserve a graceful Korean-historical silhouette and a refined updo hairstyle, but keep all details painterly and restrained.\n\nAbove and around 