two painted phoenixes overhead, and one faint fish-dragon suggested within a low ribbon of cloud-water.\n\nPreserve the queen’s hairstyle and robe silhouette from S7. Her face is unseen. No additional human figures.\n\nThe dream forms have incomplete, translucent brushstroke edges, already resembling traces of pigment rather than solid bodies. Their flowing lines gently converge toward the headrest’s decorated surface. This is a single suspended moment before their return, not a sequence of transformation stages.\n\nLeave generous ink-black negative space between the forms. The artifact is the visual anchor; the imagined world is becoming a memory.\n\nExpressive East Asian brushwork, aged pigment texture, muted crimson, warm brown, ink black, restrained ochre gold. Match the existing painted film. Tender, quiet, elegiac, 16:9 landscape, no text.\n\nS8 영상 — Higgsfield MiniMax H3\nOne continuous shot based on the supplied composite painting. Begin with a brief readable hold of the existing pillow, celestial figure, lotus, two phoenixes and fish-dragon. Keep the pillow's silhouette and painted patterns fixed. The outer edges of the figures above gradually loosen into the crimson, cream and ochre brush strokes already present, and those strokes flow gently downward toward the pillow. Let the upper composition become simpler and quieter. In the closing portion, one broad ink-black and dark-crimson brush sweep passes across the central image, briefly obscuring the pillow's outline to create a clean editing point. Maintain the aged painted texture throughout. No explosive collapse, new creatures, pillow deformation, text, speech or music.\n\nGemini 3.8 Flash TTS — 공통 연기 지시\nWarm Korean female documentary narration, natural Seoul Korean, consistent medium register and close dry recording. Calm, clear, gently curious and emotionally restrained. Read the exact text with brief natural pauses and no drawn-out endings. Finish speaking in approximately {seconds} seconds. No silence padding, no instructions spoken, no music, no whispering.\n\nGemini 3.8 Flash TTS — 장면별 입력 대사\nS1: 백제 왕비의 장례용 베개. 왜 꽃을 그렸을까요?\nS2: 붉은 문양 속에서, 왕비의 다음 길을 상상합니다.\nS3: 연꽃 한가운데, 하늘의 존재가 피어 있습니다.\nS4: 하늘을 나는 비천. 그를 길잡이 삼아, 여정을 이어갑니다.\nS5: 긴 깃을 펼친 봉황 한 쌍이, 그 여정에 함께합니다.\nS6: 용의 머리, 물고기의 몸. 어룡이 물결에 몸을 싣습니다.\nS7: 그 길 끝에서, 왕비가 연꽃을 마주합니다. 죽음 너머에도 평안하기를.\nS8: 풍경은 다시 베개의 문양으로 돌아갑니다. 좋은 세상에 다시 태어나길 빌던, 남은 이들의 마지막 배웅입니다.\n\nSuno — Style\nInstrumental underscore for a quiet Korean digital-heritage documentary about the final dream of a Baekje queen. Intimate, solemn, tender and mysterious, with restrained emotion and a sense of an ancient painted world slowly opening. Use sparse Korean traditional timbres: soft gayageum plucks, breathy daegeum phrases and a low sustained haegeum line; a very faint muted buk pulse may enter only in the middle. Let the music feel hand-played, spacious and human, with long silences between phrases. Begin almost in silence, open gently through the middle, reach a small warm emotional lift, then return to stillness and leave a long natural decay. Keep the melody simple and memorable, never grand or triumphant. Low density, soft dynamics, warm dark room tone, no sharp attacks, no busy ornamentation. Leave clear space for a calm Korean female documentary narration; keep the midrange uncluttered and avoid competing with speech. End with a clean, gentle tail suitable for a 55-second film edit.\n\nSuno — Exclude / avoid\nNo vocals, no lyrics, no choir, no spoken words, no modern pop beat, no trap drums, no trailer percussion, no huge cinematic impacts, no bright synth arpeggios, no excessive reverb wash, no stereotypical “oriental” soundtrack gestures, no sudden loud climax.",
