      "fullPrompt": "Create PART 1 (full-film 0.0–10.0) inside <<@env_1>>. Contemporary Korean heritage art film, 16:9, cinematic and restrained, shot on ARRI Alexa 35, natural motion blur, fine film grain. Bright, calm, curated contemporary museum atmosphere, like an art space rather than a history hall. The pace is calm and unhurried; every shot leaves room to breathe.\n\nFIRST AND MOST IMPORTANT: No narration, no voice, no spoken words, no subtitles, no captions, no titles and no on-screen text of any kind at any moment. If any reference contains text or voice, do not reproduce it. Audio contains room tone, music and sound effects only. Captions and voice will be added later in editing.\n\nGLOBAL LAYOUT: Use the SAME exhibition hall, architecture, tall windows, natural daylight, open stone pedestals and ceramics as <<@env_1>>. There are no glass vitrines. In the front row of three pedestals, from left to right: the celadon maebyeong vase, the blue-and-white porcelain jar, and the HERO JAR (the cheolhwa buncheong bottle with the bold iron-brown fish) on the RIGHTMOST pedestal. The ceramics and pedestals NEVER move, never overlap and are never rearranged; only the camera moves. The HERO JAR keeps its exact shape, fish motif, rough white slip and fine crackle. The painted fish never moves. The protagonist is a young Korean woman in her late twenties, seen only from behind or in soft profile, face never visible, with the same clothing and hairstyle in every shot. Other visitors appear only from behind or as soft silhouettes.\n\nLOCAL times below correspond to full-film 0.0–10.0.\n\n0–2.2 S01 THE EMPTY PLACE: Wide frontal view of the three front pedestals, exactly as in <<@env_1>>. A few visitors stand quietly in front of the celadon on the left and the blue-and-white jar in the center. The space in front of the HERO JAR on the rightmost pedestal is completely empty. The protagonist walks slowly into the frame from the left. A very slow, straight dolly-in. Sound: quiet museum room tone, soft footsteps.\n\n2.2–3.5 S02 CELADON — BLUE-GREEN LIGHT: A clean medium shot of the celadon vase on its pedestal, its whole form readable. A thin band of window light glides slowly across the glaze, and a soft jade-blue glow rises gently from within the surface, then settles. Camera still. Sound: one clear, glass-like tone with a short, soft decay.\n\n3.5–4.8 S03 BLUE-AND-WHITE JAR — EMPTY SPACE: A clean medium shot of the blue-and-white porcelain jar, placed off-center with wide, quiet empty space around it; soft diffused light on its white curve, the background falling into soft pale blur. Completely still, like a photograph. Sound: a second soft tone, then near-silence.\n\n4.8–7.8 S04 AND THEN, THE LINE: The protagonist, seen from behind, walks to the rightmost pedestal and stops in front of the HERO JAR. The HERO JAR fills the foreground. In the soft background, the visitors at the celadon and the blue-and-white jar remain as blurred silhouettes. After she stops, the soft edge of a line of window light slowly passes across the bottle, revealing the iron-brown fish strokes one by one: thick where pressed, thinning, splitting into dry brush hairs, on rough white slip with fine crackle. A very slow, straight push-in toward the bottle. Sound: footsteps stop; complete stillness.\n\n7.8–10.0 S05 INTO THE LINE: The push-in continues without a cut toward the painted fish on the HERO JAR: the big eye, then the rough iron pigment, then individual split brush hairs, until the camera enters inside a dark red-brown brushstroke, where the white slip becomes a vast terrain and the stroke rises like a ridge. Slow at first, accelerating only in the final second. Sound: a low brush-friction tone begins and swells.\n\nNo particles, no glow, no smoke, no spotlight beams, no glowing edges, no fantasy effects, no glass vitrines, no new objects, no redesign of any ceramic.\n\n\nEdit PART 2 <<@vid_25>>. FIRST AND MOST IMPORTANT: the source video contains burned-in Korean subtitles at the bottom of the frame and a spoken Korean narration voice track. REMOVE ALL OF THEM. Erase every subtitle and every piece of on-screen text completely, and fill those areas seamlessly with the surrounding image, matching light, texture, grain and motion, with no blur patches or flicker. Remove the narration voice entirely; the output must contain NO human voice and NO spoken words at any moment. Rebuild the audio with music and sound effects only.\n\nFrom the source, keep ONLY its visual style: the tactile cinematic heritage-film look, coarse gwiyal white-slip ridges, bold iron-brown strokes, loaded pressure, split dry-brush ends and handmade ceramic texture. Do NOT keep its text, its voice or its original sequence; replace the sequence and timing with the continuous journey below.\n\nReference duties: <<@img_14>> fish brushwork; <<@img_15>> peony brushwork; <<@img_16>> exaggerated bird brushwork; <<@img_13>> vine brushwork; <<@img_19>> Gyeryongsan mountain identity. Hands only, no potter face. Cracking/flaking are poetic transitions within an imagined fresh material landscape, NOT damage to a historical museum artifact.\n\nGLOBAL: No narration, no voice, no subtitles, no captions, no on-screen text of any kind at any time; music and sound effects only. In every 15th-century Joseon shot, no power lines, no electric wires, no transmission towers, no utility poles, no pylons, no modern roads, buildings or vehicles; if any appear, remove them and fill seamlessly with the surrounding sky, mist, mountains, fields or trees.\n\nBRUSH RULE (every painting shot): The brush truly PAINTS onto BLANK white slip. Before the brush arrives, the surface under it is empty; there is no pre-existing drawing beneath it. Iron-brown pigment is deposited only where the brush tip passes, and each stroke grows progressively behind the moving tip, never appearing ahead of it. The brush never rubs, smears, scrubs or traces back and forth over an already painted image. Each stroke is one forward movement: the tip touches down, the bristles bend and spread under pressure, the stroke is laid down wide and dark, thins as the pigment runs out, and the bristles split and lift off at the end. The brush and hand keep their shape.\n\nLOCAL times below correspond to full-film 10.0–32.7. Cover ALL beats; introduction 5.5s, four motifs each 3.3s, closing 4s. Final 0.3s is expendable editing handle.\n\n0–1.5 S06: Race forward inside the fish brushstroke across white-slip terrain. Its coarse bristle grain becomes the matching texture of a sea of clouds. Camera rises above clouds. Brush friction bridges to wind.\n\n1.5–3 S07a: Clouds part, dawn over the Korean peninsula, then Gongju where Geum River makes a broad bend. Accelerating vertical descent.\n\n3–4.3 S07b: SAME uninterrupted hyper-zoom, NO cut/reset: Gyeryongsan ridges → misty kiln village → through a thatched workshop roof into the interior, decelerating. Modern roads/city traces disappear during descent, arriving in a 15th-century Joseon landscape. Lowering wind, birds and kiln fire.\n\n4.3–5.5 S08: Overhead on potter's HANDS swiftly painting a NEW peony in a few strokes on a BLANK area of a white-slipped vessel; each stroke appears only as the brush lays it down. Speed-ramp; quicker strokes split at their ends. Three tactile brush swishes.\n\n5.5–7.1 S09a FISH: Extreme macro. On BLANK white clay, a visible brush lays down three rapid forward strokes, each appearing only behind the moving tip, forming a new fish. Wet iron pigment firms and fine netlike cracks spread. Fixed camera becomes minute forward tracking along cracks; three percussive strokes then tiny ticks.\n\n7.1–8.8 S09b: Crack lines become ripples. Camera falls between them beneath the morning Geum River surface. A real spotted mandarin fish crosses through shafts of light. Pan with fish; muffled underwater sound.\n\n8.8–10.2 S10a PEONY: Midday light. On BLANK white clay, the brush lays down two great petal curves and one stem, each stroke appearing only behind the moving tip. Thin hardened pigment fragments around strokes blow away, leaving just the bold essential curves. Slight oblique fixed view; crisp flaking sound.\n\n10.2–12.1 S10b: Detached fragments become peony petals blowing through a REAL peony garden. Camera follows petals from low altitude into a swift drone-like ascent over a stone wall. Wind and a single piano note.\n\n12.1–13.8 S11a BIRD: Continuing the same drone-like ascent without a cut, a small real bird takes off from the peony garden and rises steeply into the open afternoon sky above the rice fields beneath Gyeryongsan. The camera climbs beside it, matching its speed. Behind the bird, one bold iron-brown brushstroke is drawn through the air along its exact flight path, chasing it upward; the stroke's own wet, loaded leading head advances like a brush tip, thick where it presses, splitting into dry bristle ends at each turn. No floating brush object, no hand in the sky. Wingbeats, a brush swish trailing the bird, and a clarinet phrase.\n\n13.8–15.4 S11b: The chasing stroke catches up with the bird and, in a few quick decisive movements, wraps around its silhouette to draw ONE bird: one sweeping body stroke, a beak stroke MUCH longer than the real beak, and an eye dab MUCH larger than the real eye. The real bird flies on and disappears into the bright sky; only the humorous exaggerated iron-painted bird remains, perfectly still. The bright sky behind it softly turns into rough white-slip clay, so the painted bird rests on white clay and firms with fine cracks. Two brush impacts (eye, beak), playful clarinet ending, then tiny ticks. Painted bird never flies. Humorous but not cartoonish.\n\n15.4–16.8 S12a VINE: A crack starts at the bird stroke, grows vine-like across the white clay. Rapid forward tracking along its branching path. Ticks become rhythm.\n\n16.8–18.7 S12b: Camera rapidly rises into overhead view; vine-crack grain is revealed as wind-combed riverside grass at sunset. Tiny children silhouettes run along the grain. All instruments briefly join.\n\n18.7–22.7 S13: Grass lines gather into ONE long dark-red iron-brown line. Camera races along it; field becomes white-slip surface. Decelerate to extreme macro of a freshly painted wet iron line as kiln firelight brushes across it. Musical rhythm thins to fire sounds.\n\n22.7–23: Hold that same final image gently, no new event; trimming handle.\n\n\nS15 32.7–35.0 밤의 계룡산 가마. 사기장은 실루엣 수준. 장작 한 번 → 불꽃 → HERO JAR의 젖은 철화선 → 불이 가라앉는다. 불티가 느려지다 공중에 정지한다. 없음 불소리 / 음악 급감 불이 찰나의 붓질을 붙잡음\nS16 35.0–37.5 PART 1과 정확히 같은 낮 박물관. 창밖은 계속 대낮. 좌 청자 / 중앙 백자 / 우 분청사기 위치 절대 고정. 실내 전시등만 딸깍 꺼진다. 0.3초의 어둠. 우측 HERO JAR 위 스팟 하나만 켜지고, 사람 키만 한 철 안료 한 획이 공중에 정지해 있다. 없음 음악 완전 정지, room tone WOW 2. 한 획 자체가 현대 설치미술이 됨\nS17 37.5–41.0 첫 획이 깨어나며 다른 획을 하나씩 깨운다. 굵은 선은 바닥 가까이 무겁게, 가는 선은 천장으로 날카롭게, 갈라진 붓털은 튕기듯. 획이 지나간 벽·바닥 표면에는 순간적으로 백토와 귀얄 자국이 드러난다. 유리에는 반사, 벽에는 거대한 그림자. 도자기와 진열장은 절대 움직이지 않는다. 그들은 세상을 그렸습니다. 낮은 bass pulse 재개 박물관 전체가 거대한 철화분청의 표면이 됨\nS18 41.0–44.5 현대무용+서예+재즈 즉흥연주처럼 선들이 전시실 전체를 안무한다. 물고기=낮고 빠른 곡선 / 꽃=거대한 확장곡선 / 새=수직 급상승과 급격한 꺾임 / 넝쿨=공간을 묶는 연속 나선. 문양은 0.3초씩만 순간적으로 읽힌다. 절정에서 선·그림자·유리 반사가 단 하나의 앵글에서 거대한 현대 추상화 한 점으로 정렬된다. 없음 BGM 최고조 작품의 메인 WOW. 500년 전 미감의 현대 공간 확장\nS19 44.5–46.0 정점에서 모든 것이 탁— 정지. 0.2초 무음. 이후 시간 역재생처럼 획들이 정확한 경로로 되감긴다. 물고기·꽃·새·넝쿨은 각자의 실제 유물 문양으로 귀환. 바닥·벽의 백토 흔적도 사라진다. 마지막 물고기 선이 우측 HERO JAR로 들어가고 물고기 눈 극매크로에서 정지. 없음 급정지 → 붓소리 하나 퍼포먼스가 다시 유물 속으로 접힘\n\nBGM과 나레이션은 일단 제외하고 공간음 효과음 위주로 카메라 무빙 적극적으로(아트웍보일 수 있도록) \n\n분청사기는 \n을 위치 (가장 우측위치)에서 고정되어 있고 절대 벗어나지 않는다. 마지막 도자기 속 물고기 눈은 \n의 물고기 눈과 동일하게 (더 크거나 작게 생성하지 말것)",
