      "fullPrompt": "S01\n(이미지)\nEdit Image A. Use Image B only as the reference for layout, spacing, silhouette arrangement, and overall composition. Preserve the ceramic identity, glaze color, surface texture, material quality, and lighting mood of the five vessels from Image A.\nRecompose the scene so the five vessels are arranged on one single long dark museum plinth in a formal frontal lineup that follows the structure of Image B.\nArrange the vessels from left to right in this exact order:\nSanggam celadon\nIron-decorated celadon\nBuncheong\nBluish-white porcelain\nIron-painted white porcelain\nShow all five vessels in full frontal view from rim to base.\nUse the same five vessels from Image A, keeping their material identity exactly recognizable:\npale jade-gray sanggam celadon\ndark olive-brown iron-decorated celadon\ngray-white buncheong\ncool bluish-white porcelain\nwarm off-white iron-painted white porcelain body\nRearrange them to match the clear, evenly staged frontal composition of Image B:\neach vessel stands upright and fully visible\nspacing between vessels is generous and elegant\nno heavy overlap\nall five sit on one continuous plinth\nthe group reads as a refined museum display\nAdjust the overall silhouette balance to reflect the composition logic of Image B:\nthe first three vessels remain relatively slender and vertically flowing\nthe fourth and fifth vessels become visibly broader, rounder, and fuller in the body\nthe fifth vessel reads as the largest and most substantial overall\nthe fourth vessel also appears large and rounded\nthe buncheong remains slightly smaller and more compact\nthe first and second vessels remain relatively tall and narrow compared with the last two\nKeep the proportions elegant and historically plausible, with:\nsoftly swelling shoulders\ngraceful tapering lower bodies\ncalm vertical flow\nno squat or awkward distortion\nPreserve the dark black museum background and the cinematic low-key lighting mood from Image A:\ninvisible light source\nsoft directional museum lighting\nclear sculptural modeling\ngentle highlight roll-off\nreadable glaze and ceramic texture\nsubtle spatial depth\nno flat catalog lighting\nKeep the plinth as a substantial dark rectangular display base, clearly visible across the bottom of the frame.\nMaintain a refined, minimal, premium museum atmosphere.\nNo decoration, no painted motifs, no text, no signage, no people, no visible lighting equipment.\n(동영상)\nUse Image1 as the starting frame and visual reference. Preserve the exact arrangement, shapes, proportions, materials, and colors of all five ceramic vessels, along with the pedestal, background, and composition.\nThe museum lighting gradually dims and switches off in one smooth, continuous sequence. The soft frontal fill light fades first, allowing the shaded sides of the vessels to deepen. Gentle directional illumination briefly lingers across their rims, rounded shoulders, and curved sides, revealing their three-dimensional volume through soft gradients of light and shadow.\nThe remaining highlights and glaze reflections gradually soften and fade, until the vessels and pedestal disappear into darkness. Create this transition through the natural dimming of the scene’s lights, not a uniform fade-to-black overlay. Keep the light sources fixed and outside the frame, with no sweeping beams, flickering, or sudden changes.\nBy the end of the shot, all lights are completely off, leaving the frame entirely black with no remaining highlights, reflections, or visible silhouettes.\nThe camera is completely locked off. All five vessels remain motionless and unchanged. One continuous shot.\n\nS02\n(이미지)\nA photorealistic cinematic museum shot of five plain Korean ceramic vessels displayed together on one single long, low, dark museum plinth inside a nearly black contemporary exhibition space.\nThe image is a tight low-angle portrait of the upper forms of the ceramics. The frame is dominated by the rounded shoulders, necks, and mouth rims of all five vessels. Their bases and most of their lower bodies remain outside the frame.\nUse the attached five vessel reference images as the primary reference for their individual silhouettes, proportions, glaze character, surface texture, and subtle relative scale.\nVessel order\nArrange the vessels diagonally through depth in this exact order:\nSanggam celadon — closest to camera\niron-decorated celadon\nbuncheong\nbluish-white porcelain\niron-painted white porcelain — farthest from camera\nThe foreground sanggam celadon leads the composition.\nThe other four vessels recede progressively behind it.\nSpatial arrangement\nAll five vessels are displayed together on the same continuous plinth, curated as onemuseum grouping rather than as isolated objects on separate supports.\nThe vessels are positioned with generous physical distance between them, like individually spaced museum objects with visible breathing room.\nTheir positions are staggered along the depth axis.\nFrom the camera viewpoint, this depth arrangement creates selective overlap between their shoulder curves and neck silhouettes.\nThe composition should show both:\nclearly perceptible dark gaps between individual vessels\npartial overlap of adjacent shoulders and necks caused by perspective\nThe vessels must never appear physically crowded or touching.\nThe overlap should create a layered sculptural rhythm while preserving a strong sense of airand space between the objects.\nComposition\nThe five vessels occupy approximately the left two-thirds of the frame.\nThe right one-third remains uninterrupted deep-black negative space.\nThe ceramic group is anchored toward the lower-left and center-left rather than centered.\nThe upper-right portion of the frame remains especially empty.\nA thin but readable glimpse of the single long plinth is visible along the lower edge and occasionally through gaps between the vessels, making it clear that all five ceramics are resting on one shared display base.\nThe plinth establishes the museum environment without becoming a visual subject.\nFraming\nUse an extremely tight upper-body crop.\nShow primarily:\nupper shoulders\nshoulder-to-neck transitions\nnecks\nmouth rims\nDo not show complete vessel bodies.\nDo not show the bases.\nThe lower body should disappear below the bottom edge of the frame.\nThe different ceramic forms must remain recognizable through their shoulder curvature, neck proportion, rim profile, surface material, and glaze.\nCeramic silhouette\nPreserve the elegant proportions of historical Korean maebyeong and jar forms.\nThe vessels have:\ngently elongated vertical proportions\nsoftly swelling, elevated shoulders\nsmooth shoulder-to-neck transitions\nrestrained, flowing curvature\nslightly upright, buoyant silhouettes\nAvoid squat, compressed, cylindrical, or stubby forms.\nThe curves should feel full and graceful without becoming exaggerated or stylized.\nRelative scale\nKeep the differences in vessel size subtle and natural.\nThe hierarchy should be understated, not dramatic.\nThe iron-painted white porcelain at the far back should read as the largest and tallest vessel overall, with slightly greater visual mass and a slightly higher rim line than the others, even within the tight crop.\nThe bluish-white porcelain may read as somewhat large.\nThe two celadon vessels remain relatively close in scale.\nThe buncheong vessel reads slightly more compact.\nThese differences should be expressed gently through:\nslightly higher rim position\nslightly taller neck\nslightly broader shoulder mass\nslightly greater overall visual presence\nDo not exaggerate the size hierarchy.\nAll five vessels should still feel balanced as one ensemble.\nMaterials\nAll five vessels are completely plain and undecorated.\n1. Sanggam celadon\njade gray-green translucent celadon glaze, fine subtle crackle\n2. Iron-decorated celadon\nmuted olive-green to brown-green celadon glaze, slightly darker visual weight\n3. Buncheong\ngray-white body, thin white slip, subtle clay grain and handcrafted surface irregularity\n4. Bluish-white porcelain\ncool bluish-white porcelain glaze, smooth and clean\n5. Iron-painted white porcelain body\nsoft warm off-white porcelain glaze\nNo motifs.\nNo paintings.\nNo inlay.\nNo inscriptions.\nNo decorative marks.\nThe visual differences come entirely from form, clay body, glaze, tone, and surface texture.\nCamera\nUse a 24mm full-frame lens.\nPosition the camera very close to the plinth and approximately 10–15 cm above its top surface, clearly below the shoulder level of the foreground vessel.\nThe lens looks upward toward the vessels with a restrained 6–8 degree upward tilt.\nThis creates a controlled low-angle perspective in which the shoulders rise prominently above the viewer and the necks and rims are seen against the dark background.\nThe camera should feel physically low, as though the viewer is standing or crouching close to the display and looking slightly upward across the ceramics.\nMaintain natural architectural perspective.\nKeep vertical forms believable.\nAvoid extreme worm’s-eye distortion.\nAvoid dramatic convergence of vertical lines.\nAvoid excessive enlargement of the foreground vessel.\nThe 24mm lens should communicate spatial depth and separation between the five vessels, not exaggerated wide-angle distortion.\nDepth of field\nUse a deep depth of field, with the visual character of approximately f/8–f/11.\nAll five vessels remain substantially sharp from foreground to background.\nThe foreground sanggam celadon may carry the finest micro-detail, but the remainingvessels must retain clearly readable:\nrims\nneck contours\nshoulder profiles\nglaze textures\nclay surfaces\nDepth is communicated through perspective, overlap, spacing, scale, and light falloff rather than blur.\nNo strong bokeh.\nNo heavily blurred rear vessels.\nLighting\nThe exhibition environment is extremely dark.\nThe background is a pure black void with no visible architectural detail.\nControlled narrow museum spotlights fall from above and slightly behind the vessels.\nSoft grazing light traces the curved shoulders and necks, revealing:\nglaze sheen\nsubtle crackle\nclay grain\nsurface irregularity\nceramic curvature\nEach vessel receives a restrained elongated highlight.\nHighlights remain soft and controlled, never blown out.\nNo god rays.\nNo visible lamps.\nNo dramatic theatrical lighting.\nThe foreground sanggam celadon has the clearest surface definition, while the light gradually becomes softer through depth.\nOverall visual character\nPhotorealistic, ultra-realistic, cinematic museum art film, contemporary exhibition photography, tactile ceramic surfaces, refined minimalism, quiet luxury, restrained Korean aesthetic, elegant negative space, sculptural composition, contemplative atmosphere, subtle monumentality, realistic ceramic material response.\nStrict composition constraints\nFive vessels only.\nAll five displayed together on one single long plinth.\nSanggam celadon closest to camera.\nFixed vessel order through depth.\n24mm full-frame lens.\nCamera positioned 10–15 cm above the plinth surface.\n6–8 degree upward viewing angle.\nTight crop showing primarily shoulders, necks, and rims.\nLower bodies and bases outside the frame.\nGenerous physical spacing between vessels.\nSelective silhouette overlap created by staggered depth.\nDeep depth of field with all five vessels substantially sharp.\nApproximately one-third of the right side empty black negative space.\nThin glimpse of the shared plinth along the lower edge.\nGraceful elongated ceramic proportions with softly swelling shoulders.\nSubtle relative size differences only, but the farthest iron-painted white porcelain should read as the largest vessel overall.\nNo decoration.\nNo people.\nNo signage.\nNo high-angle view.\nNo top-down perspective.\nNo telephoto compression.\nNo shallow depth of field.\nNo squat vessel proportions.\nNo tightly packed arrangement.\n(동영상)\nAn extremely slow, smooth left orbit on a stabilized motion-control rig, moving at approximately 0.2x normal camera speed. The camera covers only a very small orbital arc over the entire shot, while gradually lowering in height by a subtle amount.\nThe motion is continuous, constant-speed, mechanically controlled, and nearly imperceptible from moment to moment. The lowering movement is equally gradual, with no sudden drop or change in pace.\nPerfectly stable horizon, consistent distance from the subject, no handheld feel, no micro-jitter, no sway, no bobbing, no vibration, no acceleration or deceleration.\nNatural sound only — the quiet of an empty gallery. No music.\n\nS03\n(이미지)\nUse Image1 and Image2 as the primary references for the subject, lighting mood, color tone, and overall museum environment, focusing only on the leftmost Goryeo celadon vessel.\nCreate an extreme macro close-up of the Goryeo celadon vessel.\nFrame the shot so the celadon surface completely fills the screen edge to edge, with no background, no vessel outline, and no empty space visible at all. Show only the ceramic surface itself at very close range.\nextremely shallow depth of field, 50mm lens.\n(동영상)\nThen the camera performs a slow, smooth upward move along the vessel’s body. Keep the camera close to the ceramic surface throughout the shot, maintaining the feeling of an intimate macro-to-close-up study of the celadon.\n\nS04\n(이미지)\nStraight-down extreme close-up of calm, still, shallow water, filling the entire frame. No sky, no horizon, no riverbank — only the quiet water surface. Viewed directly from above in a macro shot, focusing on the smooth reflective plane of the water, with only the faintest natural surface variation and almost no visible ripples. The water should feel serene, undisturbed, and meditative. It belongs to the Goryeo celadon world, with pale jade-green coloration, soft celadon-tinted tones, and refined glazed-like reflections. The overall mood is elegant, tranquil, and restrained. Photorealistic, highly detailed, with an extremely shallow depth of field.\n(동영상)\nThe camera performs a smooth forward tracking move parallel to the water surface, traveling straight ahead while maintaining a constant height above the stream.\nKeep the camera’s downward viewing angle completely fixed throughout the shot. The optical axis, pitch, focal length, and distance from the water surface remain constant, creating the feeling of gliding forward while continuously looking down at the stream from the same height and angle.\n\nS05\n(이미지)\nse the crane from the reference image as the subject, but depict it before spreading its wings — standing naturally and calmly with its wings folded at its sides.\nCreate a close-up portrait focused on the bird’s head, long neck, and slight upper body, so that the composition highlights the crane’s elongated neck, elegant S-curve, and refined upright posture. The framing should include the face, beak, eye, red crown patch, the full graceful curve of the neck, and a small portion of the upper torso / chest so the bird feels poised and statuesque rather than cropped too tightly.\nEmphasize the long neck and its fluid, graceful curvature as a key visual feature. The pose should feel elegant, calm, and noble.\nThe crane should not look overly perfect or artificially clean. Add natural feather texture and subtle realism — slight irregularity in the plumage, delicate layering of feathers, faint variation in whiteness, soft natural texture around the neck and chest, and a slightly lived-in, realistic surface quality that makes the bird feel like a real wild crane rather than an idealized or overly polished one.\nKeep the same misty Goryeo-celadon-inspired bamboo environment as a soft background reference, but render it heavily out of focus so it remains only as an atmospheric setting behind the bird.\nUse a shallow depth of field, with the bird’s eye, face, and front plane of the neck in crisp focus, while the background melts into soft blur.\nUse a 135mm telephoto lens for a refined portrait feel with gentle background compression.\nPhotorealistic, ultra-detailed feathers, realistic lighting, cinematic wildlife portrait, serene and elegant mood.\n(동영상)\nthe crane remains standing calmly and only makes a subtle, gentle head turn.\n\nS06\n(이미지)\nSame misty jade-green bamboo world as the reference image.\nStanding deep inside a dense bamboo grove at full adult height, looking out across a river. The river runs horizontally from the left edge of the frame to the right edge, seen from its side — the camera looks across the water, not along it. Behind the water the far bank rises, with more bamboo fading into fog.\nBamboo trunks crowd the whole foreground at many different distances — some only inches from the lens and completely soft, some further back and sharp, irregularly spaced, several cut off by the left and right edges of the frame. The river is only glimpsed in narrow slivers between them, partly hidden, never fully open.\nThe bamboo and rocks should have the texture and material quality of smooth Goryeo celadon: smooth glazed surfaces and soft celadon green tones. The ground is mostly covered with grass and low natural vegetation\nUse photorealistic textures, realistic lighting, natural atmosphere, and lifelike environmental detail. Ultra-realistic, photorealistic, immersive, highly detailed, realistic landscape photography.\nround fills only the lower third. Eye level of a standing person. No people, no animals, no birds.\n(동영상)\nUse Image1 as the starting frame and visual reference for the bamboo forest, pale celadon-green palette, soft light, mist, and stream.\nThe camera tracks very slowly and smoothly to the right at a constant speed, maintaining a fixed height and viewing direction. This is a sideways dolly movement, with no panning, turning, or zooming.\nPass bamboo trunks at varied distances from the lens. Nearby trunks glide across the foreground from right to left, softly out of focus and briefly obscuring parts of the view. Midground trunks pass at a gentler apparent speed, while distant bamboo shifts only slightly through the mist. Create natural layered parallax, gradually revealing new gaps and glimpses of the stream between the trunks.\nThrough these gaps, the background stream is subtly visible, gently trickling around the rocks with small ripples and delicate moving reflections. Keep the water movement quiet and understated, blending naturally into the forest scenery.\nNatural sound only: faint distant bird calls, delicate echoes through the humid forest air, a light breeze moving through tall bamboo, and gentle flowing water and soft ripples in the distance. No music or vocal.\n(이하 첨부 불가)",
