      "fullPrompt": "Title: 520 : 多利作\n\nGenerative AI tools used:\nOpenAI ChatGPT Image Generation\nHiggsfield Kling 3.0 Pro\nGoogle Flow Music\n\n\nCOMMON VISUAL RULES\n\nUse the silver bracelet of Queen Consort of King Muryeong and the Tomb of King Muryeong as the main historical references.\n\nThe bracelet and dragon must preserve the appearance of aged archaeological silver, including patina, small scratches, pits, handmade irregularity and sculptural relief.\n\nThe dragon is always the same silver dragon awakened from the bracelet relief.\n\nThe Tomb of King Muryeong should preserve the compact brick chamber, terracotta-brown masonry, repeating brick rhythm and warm interior atmosphere.\n\nMain color palette:\naged silver, warm amber-gold, deep indigo-black, terracotta and restrained jade-teal reflection.\n\nDuring the dragon flight sequence, use soft blush-pink and ivory lotus accents.\n\nAvoid:\ngreen slime, flesh, reptile skin, liquid metal, rubber-like deformation, oversized bracelet proportions, portals, black holes, neon effects, excessive particles, fake writing, extra symbols, logos or watermarks.\n\n\n[IMAGE GENERATION]\n\n\n1. SILVER SURFACE\n\nCreate a cinematic 16:9 extreme macro image of the ancient silver bracelet.\n\nShow aged silver with scratches, tiny pits, handmade irregularity, shallow relief and subtle patina.\n\nThe viewpoint should allow a lateral or diagonal camera skim across the surface.\n\nUse deep black space with restrained warm amber reflections.\n\nThe surface should feel archaeological and tactile, not polished modern chrome.\n\n\n2. CRAFT TRACE AND ENGRAVED LINE\n\nCreate a macro image of the same silver bracelet focusing on traces of handcraftsmanship.\n\nShow fine scratches, irregular tool marks and one thin engraved line that visually leads toward later ornamental and inscription details.\n\nThe line must be physically incised into the metal, not glowing or magical.\n\n\n3. DRAGON RELIEF\n\nCreate a close macro image of the raised dragon relief on the bracelet.\n\nFocus on a three-clawed limb, sculptural head and part of the coiled body.\n\nThe dragon still belongs to the bracelet surface but appears slightly tense, as if it may awaken.\n\n\n4. AWAKENED SILVER DRAGON\n\nCreate a cinematic keyframe where the same silver dragon begins to rise from the bracelet relief.\n\nThe head and upper body lift slightly while the lower body remains connected.\n\nPreserve the aged silver material and original sculptural identity.\n\n\n5. AIRBORNE DRAGON\n\nCreate the same silver dragon immediately after fully releasing from the bracelet.\n\nThe dragon rises forward in one elegant S-shaped curve.\n\nKeep the ancient bracelet visible below or behind as its origin.\n\nUse soft blush-pink, pale rose and ivory lotus petals with restrained warm-gold highlights.\n\nInclude one complete elegant lotus blossom in the middle or far background.\n\nThe lotus should feel refined and inspired by Korean and Baekje decorative aesthetics, not like Chinese festival decoration.\n\n\n6. DRAGON CHASE FRAME\n\nCreate a dynamic chase composition with the same silver dragon flying at speed.\n\nThe camera is close behind and slightly below the dragon.\n\nThe body creates one strong sweeping curve and the head begins turning toward the viewer.\n\nUse sparse pink and ivory lotus petals and one complete lotus blossom in the distance.\n\nThe next movement should clearly suggest a charge toward the camera.\n\n\n7. SILVER WIPE FRAME\n\nCreate an extreme close action frame where the same silver dragon is about to pass directly over the camera.\n\nIts rigid silver head, neck and body should fill approximately 85 to 95 percent of the frame.\n\nThe physical silver body should function as a natural screen wipe.\n\n\n8. SINGLE BRACELET HERO\n\nCreate one authentic ancient silver bracelet in a strong three-quarter hero view.\n\nPreserve realistic wearable proportions, band thickness, dragon relief, archaeological patina and rigid silver structure.\n\nUse a dark background with warm amber lighting.\n\n\n9. BRACELET WINDOW TO THE TOMB\n\nCreate one bracelet viewed almost front-on.\n\nIts real physical circular opening should be centered.\n\nThrough the actual empty center, show the distant interior of the Tomb of King Muryeong.\n\nThe bracelet remains a real object and the opening is not a portal.\n\nPreserve realistic bracelet thickness and rigid silver material.\n\n\n10. TOMB THRESHOLD\n\nCreate a cinematic view entering the Tomb of King Muryeong.\n\nShow the compact brick chamber, curved vault, repeating brick structure and warm terracotta-brown surfaces.\n\nUse strong architectural depth and warm amber grazing light.\n\n\n11. TOMB DRAGON FLOAT\n\nCreate a calm atmospheric image inside the Tomb of King Muryeong.\n\nOne small aged-silver dragon floats gently in the middle distance.\n\nThe tomb remains the main visual subject.\n\nThe dragon should feel serene, quiet and naturally present in the ancient space.\n\n\n12. LIVING BRICK WALL\n\nCreate a close oblique view of the tomb wall with one small silver dragon moving near the brick surface.\n\nThe bricks closest to its path should appear ready to respond.\n\nA few brick faces may look slightly more prominent and mortar shadows slightly deeper, but the wall remains structurally solid.\n\nInclude a darker mortar seam farther along the direction of travel.\n\n\n13. INNER SILVER SEARCH\n\nCreate a macro image of the inner surface of the bracelet.\n\nShow aged silver, fine scratches, engraved traces and partial character strokes.\n\nThe camera angle and curvature should prepare for the final inscription reveal.\n\nDo not reveal the full inscription clearly yet.\n\n\n14. FINAL INSCRIPTION\n\nCreate an accurate cinematic macro image of the authentic historical inscription:\n\n多利作\n\nPreserve every character stroke faithfully according to the reference.\n\nThe characters must appear physically engraved into aged silver.\n\nUse warm amber-gold grazing light to reveal the depth of the grooves.\n\n\n[VIDEO GENERATION]\n\n\nK01. SILVER SKIM\n\nBegin in near darkness with a brief restrained reflection revealing the ancient silver surface.\n\nThe camera then skims laterally and diagonally across the bracelet at close range.\n\nUse surface parallax to reveal scratches, pits, patina and handcrafted texture.\n\nAvoid flashy tunnel movement or exaggerated light streaks.\n\n\nK02. TRACE TO LINE\n\nMove slowly across traces of craftsmanship on the silver surface.\n\nFollow small irregular marks until one fine engraved line becomes the main visual path.\n\nContinue along the line so the sequence develops visually from trace to stroke to ornament.\n\n\nK03. RELIEF BREATH\n\nAnimate the dragon relief very subtly while it remains attached to the bracelet.\n\nA three-clawed limb makes a small movement and the raised body slightly tenses and lifts.\n\nThe motion should feel like breath entering an ancient metal sculpture.\n\n\nK04. DRAGON AWAKENING\n\nThe silver dragon begins rising from the bracelet relief.\n\nThe head lifts first, followed by part of the upper body.\n\nThe lower body remains connected.\n\nUse one smooth elegant movement and preserve the aged silver material.\n\n\nK05. DRAGON RELEASE\n\nCreate a 4-second release and takeoff.\n\nThe dragon begins partially attached to the bracelet.\n\nThe head and front body lift first.\n\nThe long body then uncoils in one coherent S-shaped motion.\n\nThe tail is the final part to release.\n\nBy the end, the dragon is fully airborne and moving upward and forward.\n\nIntroduce a few soft pink and ivory lotus petals near the end.\n\n\nK06. PINK LOTUS FLIGHT\n\nCreate a 5-second spectacular flight sequence.\n\nThe camera follows beside and slightly below the dragon while keeping pace with its movement.\n\nThe dragon performs one large elegant sweeping turn.\n\nIts head leads and the long body follows with believable delayed motion.\n\nPink, pale rose and ivory lotus petals move through the airflow.\n\nShow one or two complete lotus blossoms briefly during the shot.\n\nThe blossoms should have simple symmetrical layered petals and should remain secondary to the dragon.\n\nThe sequence should feel colorful, fast, elegant and refined.\n\n\nK07. DRAGON CHARGE\n\nCreate a 5-second action climax.\n\nBegin with the camera pursuing the dragon closely.\n\nThe dragon then turns decisively toward the camera.\n\nIts head leads the change of direction.\n\nDuring the turn, one complete soft-pink lotus may briefly appear behind it.\n\nIn the final section, the dragon flies directly toward the lens.\n\nIts rigid silver head, neck and body pass extremely close to the camera and fill almost the entire screen.\n\nEnd with a natural silver wipe.\n\n\nK08. SINGLE BRACELET ALIGNMENT\n\nKeep the bracelet completely rigid and stationary.\n\nMove only the camera around the bracelet.\n\nBegin from a three-quarter hero angle and gradually reposition toward a frontal view.\n\nThe real circular opening should become symmetrical and centered.\n\nBy the end, the Tomb of King Muryeong is visible through the true empty center.\n\n\nK09. THROUGH THE BRACELET\n\nMove the camera straight forward along the optical axis through the real empty center of the bracelet.\n\nThe distant tomb is already visible through the opening.\n\nAs the camera approaches, the silver rim grows naturally toward the edges of the frame.\n\nPass through the actual empty space without touching the silver.\n\nContinue the same forward movement into the tomb chamber.\n\nDo not use portal, teleportation or morphing effects.\n\n\nK09B. TOMB DRAGON FLOAT\n\nInside the Tomb of King Muryeong, show one small silver dragon drifting slowly and gracefully through the air.\n\nThe movement should be calm and unhurried.\n\nUse one gentle serpentine body motion.\n\nThe camera may drift slightly forward or sideways while maintaining a clear view of the tomb architecture.\n\nThe dragon should feel like a quiet presence inhabiting the ancient space.\n\n\nK10. TOMB RUN\n\nMove the camera forward through the tomb while staying close to walls, ceiling edges and brick surfaces.\n\nUse the architecture to guide the movement.\n\nCreate strong parallax and clear forward momentum.\n\nAvoid weightless drone-like floating or random bobbing.\n\n\nK11. DRAGON AND LIVING BRICKS\n\nThe silver dragon moves through the tomb close to the brick wall.\n\nAs it passes, only the bricks and wall areas nearest its flight path respond.\n\nSelected brick faces push outward slightly and then settle inward.\n\nNearby bricks follow with a soft delayed rhythm.\n\nMortar shadows deepen and relax and warm light pulses through the same path.\n\nThe wall should feel briefly alive, breathing or gently undulating where the dragon has just passed.\n\nThe effect must remain localized and architectural.\n\nThe wall as a whole remains structurally solid.\n\nAt the end, guide the camera toward a deep natural dark seam in the masonry.\n\n\nK12. DARK SEAM TO INNER SILVER\n\nMove directly toward the real dark mortar seam.\n\nEnter the narrow darkness between the bricks.\n\nAllow the frame to become briefly almost black.\n\nContinue forward and emerge extremely close to the inner silver surface of the bracelet.\n\nThe transition is created by darkness and camera movement only.\n\nBrick does not transform into silver.\n\n\nK13. INNER SEARCH\n\nGlide slowly along the inner silver band.\n\nShow fine scratches, engraved traces and partial character strokes moving through shallow focus.\n\nGradually reduce speed as the camera approaches the inscription area.\n\nDo not reveal the full text yet.\n\nThe final moment should feel focused and expectant.\n\n\nK14. INSCRIPTION REVEAL\n\nCreate one continuous 5-second discovery of the inscription.\n\nThe bracelet itself must remain static.\n\nThe inner silver band must not spin.\n\nUse camera motion only.\n\nMove the camera in a slow elegant arc with a very gentle roll.\n\nThe bracelet, engraved surface, background, shadows and reflections must remain one rigid unified scene.\n\nAny apparent rotation must come only from the camera viewpoint changing.\n\nBegin with the inscription partially unreadable.\n\nGradually move and shift focus toward the true inscription region.\n\nUse warm amber-gold grazing light to reveal more engraved detail.\n\nThe characters already exist physically in the silver and must not morph or assemble.\n\nBy the final section, the camera slows almost completely and resolves into the accurate inscription:\n\n多利作\n\nOnce readable, preserve every stroke exactly.\n\n\n[MUSIC GENERATION]\n\nCreate a 45-second instrumental cinematic electro-acoustic score inspired by ancient silver craftsmanship.\n\nNo vocals and no lyrics.\n\nBuild the sound world from delicate metallic taps, resonant metal strikes, fine scraping textures, brushed metal, subtle bell-like overtones, deep restrained resonance, airy movement and a sophisticated electronic pulse.\n\nOverall tempo feeling: approximately 108 to 112 BPM.\n\n0:00 to 0:05\nBegin near silence with sparse metallic detail and dark resonance.\n\n0:05 to 0:10\nIntroduce a subtle pulse as the silver dragon awakens.\n\n0:10 to 0:18\nCreate the main musical climax for the dragon flight using elegant driving metal percussion, shimmering overtones and airy motion.\n\n0:18 to 0:24\nTighten the rhythm as the camera approaches and passes through the bracelet opening.\n\nMove from bright metallic resonance into a warmer and darker tomb acoustic.\n\n0:24 to 0:31\nUse warm architectural resonance and tactile sequential percussion that can follow the dragon and reacting bricks.\n\n0:31 to 0:38\nGradually remove rhythmic layers and return to intimate metallic ticks, soft scraping and restrained sustained tones.\n\n0:38 to 0:41\nReduce the music almost to silence and leave space for three distinct metallic accents corresponding to 多, 利 and 作.\n\n0:41 to 0:45\nLet one final metallic resonance and a subtle warm harmonic tone decay into near silence.\n\nAvoid EDM, epic trailer brass, large orchestral percussion, vocals, choir, sentimental piano, stereotypical traditional Korean melodies and tourism-commercial music.",
