      "fullPrompt": "[공통 제작 조건]\n\nFormat: 16:9, 1920×1080, cinematic premium museum film, poetic fantasy, restrained photorealism, historically respectful, seamless continuous camera movement. Preserve the actual design and materials of the silver cup with bronze stand excavated from King Muryeong’s Tomb: aged silver body and lid, subtle gold lotus fitting, and green-patinated bronze stand. Keep the visual language elegant and quiet. Avoid game-trailer aesthetics, exaggerated Chinese fantasy styling, Western fantasy, excessive glowing effects, magical explosions, distorted artifacts, modern objects, logos, watermarks, and background music.\n\n\n[0–10초: 박물관의 실제 유물에서 문양으로 접근]\n\nBegin with the supplied museum-display image of the actual silver cup with bronze stand excavated from King Muryeong’s Tomb. Preserve the artifact, display case, lighting, background, proportions, engraved decoration, silver body, gold detail, and bronze stand exactly as shown in the reference image.\n\nThe camera begins with a quiet, stable museum view and performs one extremely slow, smooth cinematic push-in toward the engraved bird motif on the silver lid. Do not rotate or redesign the artifact. The surrounding museum background must remain completely stable throughout the approach.\n\nDuring the first six seconds, the artifact remains still. From approximately six seconds onward, only the fine engraved lines around the bird begin to show almost imperceptible organic movement, like breath traveling through the metal. The bird’s engraved wings and nearby curved lines shift very subtly without detaching from the surface. The movement must remain delicate and believable.\n\nBy the final moment, arrive at an extreme macro view of the authentic engraved bird motif. Preserve the actual metal scratches, patina, shallow engraved lines, and curved silver surface. The final frame must remain suitable for entering the world inside the engraving.\n\nSound effects only: quiet museum ambience, subtle room tone, and a faint silver resonance. No music, narration, voices, dramatic glow, particles, portal, or sudden transformation.\n\n\n[10–20초: 음각 속 진입과 살아난 새]\n\nContinue seamlessly from the extreme macro view of the engraved bird. The camera moves forward along the authentic engraved line as the shallow silver groove gradually becomes a physical ridge within a vast landscape.\n\nThe engraved bird slowly gains natural depth and becomes a living bird while preserving the silhouette and visual characteristics of the original Baekje motif. The living bird is species-neutral, with an ash-gray and warm ivory body, a short pointed beak, refined curved markings on its wings, and calm natural movement. It must look like a real bird interpreted from the artifact motif, not a phoenix, eagle, crane, or metal sculpture.\n\nThe bird gently opens its wings and flies forward, guiding the camera into the landscape. Reveal a monumental three-peaked mountain structure inspired by the mountain motif engraved on the cup lid: one tall central peak and two lower side peaks. The mountains appear as shallow embossed aged-silver and dark bronze relief, with irregular rounded contours, dense hammered texture, and engraved cloud-and-water lines around their lower slopes.\n\nThe sky, mist, air, distant water, and ground are realistic. Wind-shaped Korean pine trees appear naturally within the landscape. Maintain the same forward camera direction and restrained museum-film tone.\n\nSound effects only: a soft wing movement, distant wind, subtle water, and restrained metallic resonance. No music, narration, phoenix imagery, fantasy armor, glowing magic, triangular mountains, or abrupt transition.\n\n\n[20–30초: 새를 따라 사슴과 산악 세계로 이동]\n\nContinue seamlessly from the previous clip. Preserve the same bird, lighting, color palette, mountain structure, atmosphere, and camera direction.\n\nThe realistic ash-gray and ivory bird flies smoothly toward the left side of the three-peaked mountain landscape. The camera follows slightly behind and below the bird in one continuous forward movement. Do not change the bird’s appearance or scale.\n\nAs the bird passes above the reflective ground, reveal an antlered deer moving calmly through the landscape below. The deer must feel natural and dignified, with restrained movement. It is a living interpretation of the antlered deer engraved on the silver cup, not a fantasy creature.\n\nKeep the monumental three-peaked mountain clearly visible in the distance: a tall central peak with two lower side peaks, rendered as shallow aged-metal relief rather than solid metal boulders. Realistic mist flows around its base. Natural pine trees, engraved water lines, and subtle lotus motifs connect the living landscape to the artifact’s decorative structure.\n\nThe camera continues forward without cutting, following the bird while briefly observing the deer below. Use realistic earth, water, sky, and fog, with aged silver, dark bronze, and restrained gold accents.\n\nSound effects only: natural wingbeats, light wind, distant water, and soft deer footsteps. No music, narration, aggressive running, extra animals, Chinese fantasy architecture, excessive VFX, portals, or abrupt camera turns.\n\n\n[30–40초: 사슴에서 여덟 잎 연꽃으로 연결]\n\nContinue seamlessly from the previous clip. Preserve the same world, bird, deer, lighting, mountain shapes, material palette, and forward camera movement.\n\nThe guiding bird passes the antlered deer and gradually descends. The camera follows in one smooth downward arc, leaving the deer behind without an abrupt cut. The deer slows and becomes part of the distant landscape.\n\nBelow, reveal one monumental lotus passage with exactly eight clearly separated petals. The lotus is based on the eight-petaled lotus decoration beneath the finial of the silver cup lid. It must have exactly eight petals, arranged symmetrically and clearly readable from the camera angle. The petals are formed from aged silver with subtle gold highlights, fine engraved lines, slight wear, and historically restrained ornament.\n\nThe bird flies toward the center of the eight-petaled lotus. The lotus opens only slightly and naturally, creating a passage into the lower layer of the world. The camera follows the bird downward through its center.\n\nKeep the realistic sky, fog, water, earth, and pine trees. The three-peaked embossed mountains remain visible behind the scene, maintaining spatial continuity.\n\nSound effects only: soft wingbeats, passing wind, delicate metal friction as the petals open, and a deep distant resonance below. No music, narration, additional petals, seven-petaled or nine-petaled lotus, magical portal, light beam, explosion, excessive particles, or sudden cut.\n\n\n[40–50초: 연꽃 아래 세 마리 용의 세계]\n\nContinue seamlessly as the camera passes downward through the exact center of the eight-petaled lotus. Maintain the same camera direction, light, aged silver palette, bronze undertones, subtle gold highlights, and atmospheric depth.\n\nBelow the lotus, reveal exactly three elegant silver dragons moving slowly in the same circular direction. Keep exactly three dragons throughout the entire shot. Their bodies are long, slender, fluid, and based on the three dragons engraved on the body of the Baekje silver cup. Give each dragon a subtly different horn shape, reflecting the differences found in the original motifs.\n\nThe dragons are living silver beings with finely engraved surface details and restrained movement. They must feel ancient, ceremonial, and graceful rather than aggressive. Do not depict Western dragons, muscular monsters, winged dragons, or exaggerated Chinese fantasy dragons.\n\nThe camera moves between the three dragons and gradually pulls back to reveal their circular relationship beneath the eight-petaled lotus. Above them, the monumental three-peaked mountain world is faintly visible through mist, connecting the upper immortal landscape and the lower dragon layer into one continuous symbolic structure.\n\nUse realistic mist, water, depth, and controlled reflections. Emphasize the relationship between the mountains above, the lotus boundary, and the three dragons below.\n\nSound effects only: deep moving air, distant water, subtle metallic body movement, and a low silver resonance. No music, narration, battle, roaring, fire, lightning, extra dragons, missing dragons, glowing portal, excessive particles, or game-trailer effects.\n\n\n[50–60초: 세계가 문양으로 접히고 엔드카드로 귀환]\n\nContinue seamlessly from the previous clip and complete the entire ending within exactly ten seconds. Use the supplied completed end-card image as the exact final-frame reference. Preserve the same cinematic lighting, aged silver palette, bronze undertones, restrained gold accents, atmosphere, scale, and camera direction.\n\n0–3 seconds:\nFollow exactly three elegant silver dragons moving slowly in the same circular direction beneath an exactly eight-petaled lotus. Their long, slender bodies and subtly different horn shapes remain inspired by the engraved dragons on the Baekje silver cup. Their movement is calm, graceful, and ceremonial.\n\n3–6.5 seconds:\nBegin one smooth, uninterrupted pullback. The three dragons gradually lose their physical depth, becoming shallow silver relief and then delicate engraved lines. The eight-petaled lotus, clouds, three-peaked mountains, birds, deer, and trees also compress naturally into the curved metal surface.\n\nThe transformation must remain continuous and physically coherent:\nliving three-dimensional world → shallow metal relief → fine engraved motifs → complete historical artifact.\n\nContinue pulling back until the complete silver cup with bronze stand appears in a dark premium museum display. Preserve the exact historical artifact shown in the supplied reference image: original silhouette and proportions, domed silver lid, lotus-shaped finial, gold lotus fitting, rounded engraved silver bowl, cylindrical support, and wide aged bronze tray with natural green patina.\n\n6.5–7.5 seconds:\nComplete the transition into the supplied end-card image. Match its composition, artifact placement, dark background, lighting, Korean typography, spacing, and colors. The moving museum shot should gently settle into a perfectly still graphic image.\n\n7.5–10 seconds:\nHold the supplied end card completely still and fully legible. Preserve the Korean text exactly as shown in the reference image. Do not rewrite, animate, distort, add, remove, or replace any letters.\n\nAdd one calm Korean narration line from approximately 6.8 to 9.5 seconds. Use a quiet, warm, dignified museum-documentary voice with slow and natural Korean pronunciation:\n\n“한 잔에 새겨진, 백제인이 꿈꾸었던 세계.”\n\nSound effects: soft distant wind and water during the dragon scene, a delicate silver resonance as the world becomes engraved lines, and one restrained metallic chime as the end card settles into place. Allow the chime and narration to fade naturally into silence.\n\nNo background music, singing, chanting, or additional voices. Avoid extra or missing dragons, different dragon directions, extra lotus petals, phoenix imagery, portals, explosions, magical beams, excessive particles, abrupt cuts, altered artifact proportions, invented ornament, missing bronze stand, modified end-card layout, misspelled Korean, warped letters, new text, logos, or watermarks.",
