      "fullPrompt": "** 크낙새 이미지 **\nBIRD REFERENCE SHEET — @char_keunaksae\nWildlife reference sheet for a natural-history documentary film, single image, five views of the SAME individual bird arranged on one sheet. FIVE separate panels: exactly three panels of identical size in the top row and exactly two panels of identical size in the bottom row, no panel merging. Each panel contains ONLY this one bird. Neutral seamless mid-gray studio background, even soft lighting from both sides, thin light-gray dividing lines between views, small English caption under each view. Where the bird clings, it grips a short vertical section of gray-brown oak bark post, the only prop on the sheet.\n\nTOP ROW — three views, identical bird scale:\n(1) SIDE PROFILE, CLINGING — exact 90-degree side view, body vertical against the bark post, stiff tail feathers braced on the bark, head and bill pointing toward the right edge of the panel.\n(2) THREE-QUARTER FRONT, CLINGING — rotated 45 degrees toward the camera, showing the breast and belly and both sides of the head.\n(3) BACK VIEW, CLINGING — seen from directly behind, wings folded, the white rump patch clearly visible between the black wings.\n\nBOTTOM ROW — two views:\n(4) FLIGHT, UNDERSIDE — wings fully spread, seen from below, showing the white belly and the white underwing linings contrasting with the black flight feathers.\n(5) HEAD DETAIL — tight side portrait of the head only: crimson crest and peaked nape tuft, crimson moustache stripe, pale straw-yellow iris with a catch-light, dark slate chisel bill.\n\nIdentical plumage pattern, head markings and proportions across all five views — one bird captured from five angles, not five different birds.\n\nIDENTITY: Adult male White-bellied Woodpecker of the Korean subspecies (Korean name Keunaksae), a very large woodpecker about 46 centimeters long, roughly the length of a grown man's forearm from elbow to fingertip. Glossy jet-black plumage on the head, throat, upper breast, back, wings and long stiff wedge-shaped tail, with a faint blue-black sheen where light strikes. Clean bright white lower breast and belly, and a white rump patch that shows between the folded black wings. Deep crimson crest covering the forehead, crown and nape, lifting into a short peaked tuft at the back of the head, and a crimson moustache stripe running back from the base of the bill across the cheek. Pale straw-yellow iris with a black pupil. Long straight chisel-tipped bill in dark slate gray. Slate-gray feet with two toes forward and two toes back.\n\nPhotorealistic natural-history reference photography, feather-level and bark-level detail, true natural color, sharp focus throughout. 16:9 horizontal frame.\n\n**까막딱다구리 이미지**\nBIRD REFERENCE SHEET — @char_kkamak\n\nWildlife reference sheet for a natural-history documentary film, single image, five views of the SAME individual bird arranged on one sheet. FIVE separate panels: exactly three panels of identical size in the top row and exactly two panels of identical size in the bottom row, no panel merging. Each panel contains ONLY this one bird. Neutral seamless mid-gray studio background, even soft lighting from both sides, thin light-gray dividing lines between views, small English caption under each view. Where the bird clings, it grips a short vertical section of gray-brown oak bark post, the only prop on the sheet.\n\nTOP ROW — three views, identical bird scale:\n(1) SIDE PROFILE, CLINGING — exact 90-degree side view, body vertical against the bark post, stiff tail feathers braced on the bark, head and bill pointing toward the right edge of the panel.\n(2) THREE-QUARTER FRONT, CLINGING — rotated 45 degrees toward the camera, showing the breast and belly and both sides of the head.\n(3) BACK VIEW, CLINGING — seen from directly behind, wings folded, the rump the same coal black as the back and wings.\n\nBOTTOM ROW — two views:\n(4) FLIGHT, UNDERSIDE — wings fully spread, seen from below, showing the belly and the underwings in the same uniform coal black as the rest of the body.\n(5) HEAD DETAIL — tight side portrait of the head only: black forehead and front crown, small crimson patch only at the rear of the crown, pale ivory-white iris with a catch-light, pale ivory-bone bill with a dark gray tip.\n\nIdentical plumage pattern, head markings and proportions across all five views — one bird captured from five angles, not five different birds.\n\nIDENTITY: Adult female Black Woodpecker (Korean name Kkamak-ttakttaguri), a very large woodpecker about 45 centimeters long, roughly the length of a grown man's forearm from elbow to fingertip. Uniform coal-black plumage over the entire body: head, throat, breast, belly, back, rump, wings, underwings and long stiff wedge-shaped tail are all the same deep black with a soft matte sheen. Forehead and front of the crown black; a single small crimson patch sits only at the rear of the crown, on the nape. Pale ivory-white iris with a black pupil. Long straight chisel-tipped bill in pale ivory-bone color with a darker gray tip. Slate-gray feet with two toes forward and two toes back.\n\nPhotorealistic natural-history reference photography, feather-level and bark-level detail, true natural color, sharp focus throughout. 16:9 horizontal frame.\n\n** C#01 - C#02 프롬프트 **\nSCENE CONTEXT\nEarly-summer dawn over an old-growth forest in central Korea that hides a Joseon-dynasty royal tomb. The camera dives from high above the canopy down through the mist and arrives at eye level among the tomb's granite statues. The scene is empty of people and animals. From deep in the forest, a woodpecker knocks on wood in a slow, steady rhythm.\n\nACTIVE REFERENCES\n<<<83206166-989e-412b-8ea9-08338be877d2>>> — old-growth corridors of tall oak, hornbeam and Korean fir trunks, fern floor, mist, slanting sun beams; from the air an unbroken dome of canopy. 100% matches the reference.\n<<<c7fc9ba5-6f92-4c31-a84a-5bec460f979b>>> — low round turf-covered burial mound on a grass terrace, paired lichen-spotted granite statues of civil officials in court robes, dense wall of old-growth forest behind. 100% matches the reference.\n\nLOCATION MAP\nThe tomb sits in a grass clearing ringed by forest. The sun is low behind the forest ridge. Mist at 30 percent density pools in the clearing and hangs in the canopy. The camera path runs from 120 meters above the canopy, down through the clearing's open sky, to 1.2 meters above the grass, stopping behind the left statue.\n\nFIRST FRAME / BLOCKING\nHigh oblique aerial view: an unbroken dome of green canopy with mist in the hollows; the small grass clearing with the round mound sits in the lower center of the frame.\n\nFORMAT MODE\nTimed multishot. Cuts only at the specified points, the camera does not cut on its own.\n\nOPTICS\n0.0s to 9.0s — Continuous aerial descent, 84 degrees wide, rectilinear.\n9.0s to 15.0s — MS, 29 degrees, shallow depth of field.\nNo drift mid-segment.\n\nCAMERA\nSegment 1: one continuous move. The camera flies forward and down along a smooth curve at 30 km/h, from 120 meters high toward the clearing, tilting from 45 degrees down to level as it descends; it sinks through the mist layer at 5.0s, arrives 1.2 meters above the grass behind the left statue at 8.0s, eases gently and comes to a complete rest at 8.5s, then holds until the cut.\nSegment 2: locked, 1.5 meters from the statue's shoulder.\n\nACTION\n0.0s to 9.0s — The canopy rushes closer; treetops sway; the camera passes through a veil of mist, and the clearing opens up: the mound, the pair of granite statues facing each other, the forest wall rising behind them. First sun rays break over the ridge at 7.0s.\n9.0s HARD CUT\n9.0s to 15.0s — The lichen-spotted stone shoulder and head of a civil-official statue fill the left third of the frame in sharp focus, facing right. At 11.0s the focus shifts smoothly from the stone to the dark forest wall in the background, where the knocking comes from. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\nPHYSICS\nMist parts and swirls in the camera's wake. Treetops bend in a light wind. Stone stays solid and still.\n\nLIGHTING\nNatural dawn light only. Contre-jour: the low sun backlights the mist and rims the statue in pale gold. Shadows soft blue-green, highlights pale gold. White balance 5600K.\n\nAUDIO\nSound effects only — natural diegetic sound from the scene, no music, no score, no voice: rushing air during the descent, softening to dawn birdsong; slow, steady woodpecker knocks from deep in the forest, about one knock per second, growing slightly clearer after the cut.\n\nSTYLE\nPhotorealistic natural-history documentary cinematography, feather-level and bark-level detail, fine film grain, 8K detail.\n\nOUTPUT SETTINGS\n24fps real time throughout. Clean, text-free image.\n\nPOSITIVE LOCKS\nThe frame shows only forest, mist, grass and stone in both segments. The statues stay stone and motionless. One single unbroken image in every segment. The soundtrack is pure natural sound effects from start to end. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\n** C#3 - C#6 프롬프트 **\nSCENE CONTEXT\nEarly-summer morning in an old-growth forest in central Korea. A male Keunaksae woodpecker clings to a massive old oak about 1 meter below his nest cavity and chisels at the bark. A rhythmic montage of tight details, every cut landing on a strike, then one wide reveal.\n\nACTIVE REFERENCES\n<<<75fae2ca-3e8c-4739-a315-e614b8169f5c>>> — adult male, very large woodpecker, glossy jet-black body with white lower breast, white belly and white rump patch, deep crimson crest from forehead to nape and crimson moustache stripe, pale straw-yellow iris, dark slate chisel bill. 100% matches the reference.\n<<<ccc06d1a-751d-4f8e-b34d-fe8bc798dd82>>> — massive deep-furrowed gray-brown oak trunk, oval nest cavity about the size of an open palm with a fresh pale tan rim, 8 meters above the ground. 100% matches the reference.\n\nLOCATION MAP\nThe oak stands at the edge of a small fern clearing; the nest cavity faces the clearing 8 meters up; the bird clings 7 meters up on the edge of the trunk. Sun beams slant from the upper left behind the tree. Mist at 30 percent density. Cameras stand on the shadow side.\n\nFIRST FRAME / BLOCKING\nExtreme close view in profile: the dark slate bill striking the bark, pointing toward the RIGHT, chips in the air.\n\nFORMAT MODE\nTimed multishot. Cuts only at the specified points, the camera does not cut on its own. Each of the first four HARD CUTs lands exactly on the moment of a strike.\n\nOPTICS\n0.0s to 2.0s — ECU of the bill, 12 degrees.\n2.0s to 3.5s — ECU of falling chips, 12 degrees.\n3.5s to 5.5s — ECU of the eye and crest, 12 degrees.\n5.5s to 7.0s — ECU of feet and tail, 12 degrees.\n7.0s to 15.0s — Wide shot from the clearing, 63 degrees.\nNo drift mid-segment.\n\nCAMERA\nSegments 1 to 4: locked, level with the bird, 1 meter away.\nSegment 5: 4 meters high, 20 meters from the trunk; slides sideways from left to right exactly 2 meters between 7.0s and 13.0s, easing to a complete rest at 13.0s, then locked.\n\nACTION\n0.0s to 2.0s — The bill hammers the bark in rapid, forceful bursts, about four strikes per second; each strike is a sharp, lightning-fast snap of the head with natural motion blur on the bill; pale tan chips burst off the tip.\n2.0s HARD CUT\n2.0s to 3.5s — Pale chips tumble down past the deep bark ridges and flash as they cross a sun beam.\n3.5s HARD CUT\n3.5s to 5.5s — The pale straw-yellow eye in profile with a bright catch-light; the whole head jolts with each rapid strike, the crimson crest feathers shaking.\n5.5s HARD CUT\n5.5s to 7.0s — Gray feet clench the bark ridges; the stiff black tail presses into the trunk, jolting with each rapid strike.\n7.0s HARD CUT\n7.0s to 15.0s — The whole oak from roots to broken top, ferns in front, sun beams through mist. The bird is a small black shape with a bright crimson point at its head, body about one-fifteenth of the frame height, striking in quick bursts; at 12.0s he stops and hitches upward in two quick, jerky hops toward the cavity. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\nPHYSICS\nReal wildlife speed: woodpecker strikes are fast, hard and percussive, the head moving like a hammer. Chips fall at true gravity speed, spinning. Each strike moves the head only; the body stays anchored by feet and tail.\n\nLIGHTING\nNatural morning light only. Sun from the upper left rims the crest in glowing crimson and edges the black feathers in silver. White balance 5600K.\n\nAUDIO\nSound effects only — natural diegetic sound from the scene, no music, no score, no voice: crisp woody knocks in sync with every strike, rapid bursts of about four knocks per second, very close and hard in the detail segments, small and distant in the wide segment; forest ambience; birdsong.\n\nSTYLE\nPhotorealistic natural-history documentary cinematography, feather-level and bark-level detail, fine film grain, 8K detail.\n\nOUTPUT SETTINGS\n24fps, true real-time speed in every segment, natural wildlife documentary playback. Clean, text-free image.\n\nPOSITIVE LOCKS\nEvery movement plays at true real-time speed. One bird, the same male Keunaksae, in every segment: crimson crest, crimson moustache stripe, white belly and rump, black body. In the profile segments his head points RIGHT, into the trunk. One single unbroken image in every segment. The soundtrack is pure natural sound effects from start to end. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\n** C#7 - C#10 프롬프트 **\nSCENE CONTEXT\nEarly-summer morning in an old-growth forest in central Korea. The male Keunaksae flies fast through the corridors between ancient trunks, and the camera chases him from behind; then he swoops overhead. Everything plays at true real-time speed.\n\nACTIVE REFERENCES\n<<<75fae2ca-3e8c-4739-a315-e614b8169f5c>>> — adult male, very large woodpecker, glossy jet-black body with white lower breast, white belly and white rump patch, deep crimson crest from forehead to nape and crimson moustache stripe, pale straw-yellow iris, dark slate chisel bill. 100% matches the reference.\n<<<83206166-989e-412b-8ea9-08338be877d2>>> — old-growth corridors of tall oak, hornbeam and Korean fir trunks, fern floor, mist, slanting sun beams; from the air an unbroken dome of canopy. 100% matches the reference.\n\nLOCATION MAP\nTall straight trunks spaced 4 to 8 meters apart form long open corridors; fern floor; mist at 40 percent density; sun beams slanting from the upper left.\n\nFIRST FRAME / BLOCKING\nFrom 2 meters behind the bird at his height: his black back, beating wings and white rump at the center of the frame, flying away from the camera down a corridor of trunks.\n\nFORMAT MODE\nTimed multishot. Cuts only at the specified points, the camera does not cut on its own.\n\nOPTICS\n0.0s to 8.5s — Chase shot, 84 degrees wide.\n8.5s to 15.0s — Low-angle upward shot, 63 degrees.\nNo drift mid-segment.\n\nCAMERA\nSegment 1: the camera flies 2 meters behind the bird at 25 km/h, matching his path as he weaves between trunks, banking gently into each turn, trunks whipping past on both sides.\nSegment 2: 0.5 meters above the forest floor, pointing up at 70 degrees, locked.\n\nACTION\n0.0s to 8.5s — Bounding woodpecker flight: three strong wingbeats, a short swooping dip with wings folded, three wingbeats again. The white rump flashes on every beat. He swerves left around one trunk at 3.0s and right around another at 6.0s, bursting through a sun beam at 7.5s.\n8.5s HARD CUT\n8.5s to 15.0s — Real time: at 9.0s he enters from the LEFT edge and swoops overhead at 25 km/h, one quick glide with wings spread and one fast wingbeat, white belly and white underwing linings flashing against the dark canopy and sun beams, and exits the RIGHT edge at 10.5s. Two leaves he brushed flutter down. The canopy and beams hold to the end of the clip.\n\nPHYSICS\nReal wildlife speed: quick, snappy wingbeats with natural motion blur on the wingtips; the body rises sharply on each wingbeat and drops on each fold. Wingbeats stir the mist into small eddies.\n\nLIGHTING\nNatural morning light only. The bird flashes in and out of the sun beams from the upper left. White balance 5600K.\n\nAUDIO\nSound effects only — natural diegetic sound from the scene, no music, no score, no voice: rushing air and quick wing whoosh on each beat, trunks passing with soft whooshes, forest ambience, birdsong.\n\nSTYLE\nPhotorealistic natural-history documentary cinematography, feather-level and bark-level detail, fine film grain, 8K detail.\n\nOUTPUT SETTINGS\n24fps, true real-time speed in both segments, natural wildlife documentary playback. Clean, text-free image.\n\nPOSITIVE LOCKS\nEvery movement plays at true real-time speed. One bird, the same male Keunaksae, in both segments, white rump and white belly clearly visible. In segment 2 he travels from the LEFT side of the frame toward the RIGHT side. One single unbroken image in every segment. The soundtrack is pure natural sound effects from start to end. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\n** C#11- C#13 프롬프트 **\nSCENE CONTEXT\nLate-autumn morning at the same weathered oak. Seen first from inside the empty nest cavity: a female Black Woodpecker lands at the entrance and looks in. Then she begins to chisel fresh wood at the rim.\n\nACTIVE REFERENCES\n<<<53402cea-530f-4c19-849a-723080891803>>> — adult female Black Woodpecker, very large, uniform coal-black body including belly, rump and underwings, a single small crimson patch only at the rear of the crown, pale ivory-white iris, pale ivory-bone bill with a dark gray tip. 100% matches the reference.\n<<<ddde9672-1f31-41a1-bd00-0d14da8e1fc9>>> — the same massive oak in late autumn, cavity rim weathered silver-gray and rounded, bright green moss cushion on its lower lip, thin torn cobweb across its upper edge, small tan bracket fungus below, a jagged broken branch stub on the left side of the trunk 5 meters up, moss on the lower trunk, slender young trees about 10 meters tall filling the clearing, golden-amber autumn leaves. 100% matches the reference.\n\nLOCATION MAP\nThe weathered cavity faces a leaf-carpeted clearing 8 meters up. Segment 1 camera sits deep inside the cavity looking out through the oval entrance. Low warm sun from the upper left, golden leaves falling, mist at 20 percent density.\n\nFIRST FRAME / BLOCKING\nFrom inside the cavity: dark raw-wood walls frame a bright oval of golden autumn light at the center; the mossy lower lip and the cobweb edge are silhouetted; beyond, the golden clearing out of focus.\n\nFORMAT MODE\nTimed multishot. Cuts only at the specified points, the camera does not cut on its own.\n\nOPTICS\n0.0s to 6.0s — Inside-cavity POV, 63 degrees.\n6.0s to 9.0s — ECU of the bill at the rim, 12 degrees.\n9.0s to 15.0s — MS from a 45-degree low angle, 29 degrees.\nNo drift mid-segment.\n\nCAMERA\nSegment 1: locked inside the cavity, 25 centimeters behind the entrance.\nSegment 2: locked, level with the rim, 0.8 meters away.\nSegment 3: locked, 4 meters below and 5 meters out from the trunk, looking up at 45 degrees.\n\nACTION\n0.0s to 6.0s — The bright oval holds. At 1.5s a dark shape swoops past below the lip with a wing whoosh and lands. At 3.0s her black head rises into the oval, backlit, and turns: the pale ivory eye, the pale ivory bill, the small crimson patch at the back of her crown catching the sun. She peers into the cavity, straight toward the camera, and holds for a beat.\n6.0s HARD CUT\n6.0s to 9.0s — Profile, bill pointing RIGHT: she strikes the weathered rim, about two strikes per second; fresh cream-white chips burst from under the silver-gray surface.\n9.0s HARD CUT\n9.0s to 15.0s — From below: she clings at the cavity rim, all-black body against the weathered trunk and golden crown, striking steadily; fresh chips spin down through the sun beams with falling golden leaves. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\nPERFORMANCE\nCurious and alive: quick head turns, a long searching look into the cavity, then energetic strikes.\n\nPHYSICS\nFresh chips fall with real weight. Feet grip the rim; the stiff tail braces against the bark. Each strike moves the head only.\n\nLIGHTING\nNatural late-autumn morning light only. Segment 1 is strong contre-jour, the head silhouetted with a warm rim. Low sun from the upper left rims the black plumage in amber and lights the fresh chips cream-white. White balance 5600K.\n\nAUDIO\nSound effects only — natural diegetic sound from the scene, no music, no score, no voice: wing whoosh at 1.5s, claws on bark, a soft breath of air in the cavity, then clear woody knocks in sync with every strike, two per second, steady and alive; birdsong returning.\n\nSTYLE\nPhotorealistic natural-history documentary cinematography, feather-level and bark-level detail, fine film grain, 8K detail.\n\nOUTPUT SETTINGS\n24fps real time throughout. Clean, text-free image.\n\nPOSITIVE LOCKS\nOne bird, the same female Black Woodpecker, in all segments: entirely black body — black belly, black rump, black underwings — with the only red a small crimson patch at the back of the crown, and a pale ivory bill. The cavity stays weathered with moss and cobweb. One single unbroken image in every segment. The soundtrack is pure natural sound effects from start to end. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\n** 엔딩 컷 프롬프트 **\nSCENE CONTEXT\nLate-autumn morning. The female Black Woodpecker keeps striking at the rim of the weathered cavity while the camera rises from the tree into the sky, revealing the whole golden forest and the royal tomb clearing beyond it.\n\nACTIVE REFERENCES\n<<<53402cea-530f-4c19-849a-723080891803>>> — adult female Black Woodpecker, very large, uniform coal-black body including belly, rump and underwings, a single small crimson patch only at the rear of the crown, pale ivory-white iris, pale ivory-bone bill with a dark gray tip. 100% matches the reference.\n<<<ddde9672-1f31-41a1-bd00-0d14da8e1fc9>>> — the same massive oak in late autumn, cavity rim weathered silver-gray and rounded, bright green moss cushion on its lower lip, thin torn cobweb across its upper edge, small tan bracket fungus below, a jagged broken branch stub on the left side of the trunk 5 meters up, moss on the lower trunk, slender young trees about 10 meters tall filling the clearing, golden-amber autumn leaves. 100% matches the reference.\n<<<83206166-989e-412b-8ea9-08338be877d2>>> — old-growth corridors of tall oak, hornbeam and Korean fir trunks, fern floor, mist, slanting sun beams; from the air an unbroken dome of canopy. 100% matches the reference.\n\nLOCATION MAP\nThe oak stands at the edge of a leaf-carpeted clearing inside a golden autumn forest; about 400 meters away, the round turf mound of the royal tomb sits in its own clearing. Low warm sun from the upper left. Mist at 20 percent density.\n\nFIRST FRAME / BLOCKING\nFrontal long view of the whole oak from roots to broken top, golden crown, the bird a small black shape at the cavity rim.\n\nFORMAT MODE\nTimed multishot. Cuts only at the specified points, the camera does not cut on its own.\n\nOPTICS\n0.0s to 4.0s — Frontal long shot, 47 degrees.\n4.0s to 15.0s — Rising shot, 63 degrees, ending as a bird's-eye view.\nNo drift mid-segment.\n\nCAMERA\nSegment 1: 1.5 meters high, 25 meters from the trunk, locked.\nSegment 2: starts 8 meters high, 6 meters in front of the cavity, level. Rises straight up at a steady 18 km/h to 60 meters between 4.0s and 12.5s, tilting down smoothly so that it points straight down by 12.0s, easing to a complete rest at 12.5s, then perfectly locked to the end.\n\nACTION\n0.0s to 4.0s — The bird strikes steadily, its body about one-fifteenth of the frame height; fresh chips glint as they fall; golden leaves drift down.\n4.0s HARD CUT\n4.0s to 15.0s — The rise begins at the bird striking at the cavity rim, passes up the trunk, through the golden crown and out above the canopy; the view turns straight down on a vast dome of golden forest, the oak's crown at the center and the tomb clearing visible near the top edge of the frame. The shot holds to the end of the clip.\n\nPHYSICS\nLeaves fall with light flutter; the canopy sways in a gentle wind as the camera clears it.\n\nLIGHTING\nNatural late-autumn morning light only, low warm sun from the upper left, long shadows across the canopy. White balance 5600K.\n\nAUDIO\nSound effects only — natural diegetic sound from the scene, no music, no score, no voice: woody knocks, two per second, steady through the whole clip, fading with distance as the camera rises; wind over the canopy.\n\nSTYLE\nPhotorealistic natural-history documentary cinematography, feather-level and bark-level detail, fine film grain, 8K detail.\n\nOUTPUT SETTINGS\n24fps real time throughout. Clean, text-free image.\n\nPOSITIVE LOCKS\nOne bird, the same female Black Woodpecker with an entirely black body, keeps striking at the rim of the weathered cavity. The knocking rhythm stays steady to the last frame. One single unbroken image in every segment. The soundtrack is pure natural sound effects from start to end. The shot holds to the end of the clip.",
