      "fullPrompt": "C#1.\n\nOne continuous unbroken drone shot, 12 seconds, no cuts.\n\nCRITICAL: The burial mounds are fixed, solid ancient structures. Their shape, size, height, number and positions must remain exactly the same as in the starting frame for the entire 12 seconds. They never grow, shrink, move, merge, split, appear or disappear. Only the camera moves; the terrain is completely static. Seasonal changes affect only the grass color, trees, lighting and weather, never the ground or the mounds.\n\nCamera path: the camera turns to the RIGHT (clockwise) throughout the shot, never to the left. It glides forward along the row of mounds while steadily rotating its view to the right, following the row as it curves toward the right side of the ridge. On screen, the landscape slides from right to left: the nearby mounds pass by on the LEFT edge of the frame one after another, while new mounds, trees and the distant hill on the RIGHT side of the frame gradually move into the center. Low altitude, smooth, stabilized, constant speed, a gentle clockwise arc like a drone tracing the curved path of the tombs.\n\nThe seasons change continuously while the camera keeps moving, each transition sweeping gradually across the landscape, not as a cut:\n0-3s: spring. Fresh green grass, pink cherry blossoms on the ridge, petals drifting in soft morning light.\n3-6s: summer. The grass turns deep lush green, the trees grow dense and dark green, bright midday sun with strong shadows.\n6-9s: autumn. The foliage turns red, orange and gold, the grass turns dry and golden, falling leaves drift across the frame, warm low sunlight.\n9-12s: winter. A thin layer of snow dusts the hillside, following the exact contours of the mounds without changing their shape, the trees are bare, gentle snowfall, cold blue dusk light.\n\nPhotorealistic, cinematic, serene and timeless atmosphere. The camera always turns right, following the mounds. The mounds stay perfectly stable and unchanged in every frame. No people, no text.\n\n---------\nC#2.\n\nOne continuous shot, 18 seconds, no cuts. High-angle aerial drone view of a snow-covered hillside at night, with many low ancient burial mounds under the snow.\n\nThe very first frame is exactly the starting image at its full normal brightness. No fade-in, no black screen, no darkening at the beginning.\n\nCRITICAL: The burial mounds are fixed, solid ancient structures. Their shape, size, number and positions must remain exactly the same as in the starting frame for the entire 18 seconds. Only the snow, light and camera change; the mounds never move, grow, shrink or disappear.\n\nTHE CORE MOUND: the largest round mound near the top center of the hillside. This single mound is the heart of the whole shot: it is the first to glow, the center of the constellation, and the target of the final zoom.\n\n0-3s: heartbeat. The camera rises very slowly. Deep inside the core mound, a soft warm golden light begins to glow through the snow, pulsing slowly like a heartbeat. It is the only mound glowing; all other mounds stay dark.\n\n3-8s: ripple. The light spreads outward from the core mound like a slow ripple, one mound at a time: first the mounds closest to the core light up, then the next ring of mounds farther away, then the outermost mounds at the edges of the hillside. Each mound lights up clearly after the previous one, never all at once, like a wave of light traveling across the hillside. As each mound lights up, the snow around it melts away in a soft circle, revealing the dark grass and the exact shape of the mound beneath, with faint mist rising. The camera keeps rising slowly and tilts down toward a top-down view.\n\n8-14s: constellation. Thin threads of golden light stretch across the ground, radiating out from the core mound to the other mounds and connecting them one by one, forming a glowing network like a vast constellation drawn on the earth. Fine sparkles of golden dust drift along the lines. The camera holds high above, showing the whole pattern.\n\n14-15s: the complete constellation glows steadily. Every line of light stays fully visible and connected; nothing fades or disappears. The core mound shines as the brightest point at the center of the network.\n\n15-18s: quick zoom. With all the lines of light still glowing on the ground, the camera suddenly dives straight down toward the core mound in a fast, accelerating push-in. The glowing lines stream past the edges of the frame as the camera plunges in, and the golden light of the core mound fills the frame until the screen is flooded in brilliant gold.\n\nSmooth, stabilized, no rotation. Majestic, sacred, mysterious, photorealistic, cinematic. No people, no text.\n\n---------\nC#3. \n\nTwo shots in one video, 8 seconds total, with one hard cut. The reference image shows the exact design of the Baekje gilt-bronze cap and the scene style: a dark ancient stone tomb chamber with a warm golden rim light.\n\nCRITICAL: The gilt-bronze cap is a rigid ancient metal object. Its shape, openwork dragon patterns, the two cup-shaped ornaments on stalks, the tall spiked tube ornament, the leaf-shaped plate and all proportions match the reference image exactly in every frame and in both shots. It never bends, melts, morphs or changes design. The air around the cap stays clean and dark, with no floating particles, lines or sparkles.\n\nShot 1 (0-4s): orbit shot.\nCamera: the camera clearly orbits around the cap in a smooth arc from left to right, about 30 degrees, keeping the cap centered, while slowly pushing in. The viewing angle visibly changes: the shot starts on the cap's front-left side and ends on its front-right side. The stone block walls in the background shift noticeably behind the cap, showing strong parallax.\nAction: the golden cap floats in mid-air, starting just slightly below the center of the frame, and gently lifts upward by only a small distance, a subtle, weightless levitation as if awakening from a long sleep, easing to a soft stop in the center. Its golden glow warms slightly as it rises. The cap itself does not rotate; only the camera moves around it. A soft golden light moves across the cap, the reflections sliding over its polished surface as the angle changes.\n\nCut to Shot 2 (4-8s): extreme close-up of the same cap. The camera glides slowly and smoothly across the surface, from the intricate openwork dragon and arabesque patterns up toward the two cup-shaped ornaments at the top. Warm golden light sweeps across the mirror-like polished gilt-bronze, sliding over the openwork and making the metal flash and shimmer. The tiny round gold spangles tremble and sparkle one after another in shallow depth of field, the dark background softly blurred.\n\nMajestic, sacred, awe-inspiring, photorealistic, cinematic. No people, no text.\n\n---------\nC#4.\n\nTwo shots in one video, 8 seconds total, with one hard cut. The reference image shows the exact design of the pair of Baekje gilt-bronze shoes and the scene style: a dark ancient stone tomb chamber with a warm golden rim light.\n\nCRITICAL: The gilt-bronze shoes are rigid ancient metal objects. Their shape, the interlocking stepped cross openwork on the sides, the diamond lattice sole, the rivets and all proportions match the reference image exactly in every frame and in both shots. Short pointed spikes exist only on the underside of the sole, pointing downward; the side walls and upper edges are smooth with nothing protruding from them. The shoes never bend, melt, morph or change design. There are always exactly two shoes. The air around the shoes stays clean and dark, with no floating particles, lines or sparkles.\n\nShot 1 (0-4s): orbit shot.\nCamera: the camera clearly orbits around the shoes in a smooth arc from right to left, about 30 degrees, keeping the shoes centered, while slowly pushing in. The viewing angle visibly changes: the shot starts on the shoes' front-right side and ends on their front-left side. The stone block walls in the background shift noticeably behind the shoes, showing strong parallax.\nAction: the pair of golden shoes floats side by side in mid-air, starting just slightly below the center of the frame, and gently lifts upward together by only a small distance, a subtle, weightless levitation as if awakening from a long sleep, easing to a soft stop in the center. Their golden glow warms slightly as they rise. The shoes themselves do not rotate; only the camera moves around them. A soft golden light moves across the shoes and shines through the openwork, the reflections sliding over their polished surfaces as the angle changes.\n\nCut to Shot 2 (4-8s): extreme close-up of the same shoes. The camera glides slowly and smoothly along the side of one shoe, following the repeating stepped cross openwork from the rounded toe toward the heel. Warm golden light shines through the openwork holes, casting delicate patterned beams, and sweeps across the mirror-like polished gilt-bronze, making the metal flash and shimmer. The smooth side wall and the small rivets catch the light in shallow depth of field, the dark background softly blurred.\n\nMajestic, sacred, awe-inspiring, photorealistic, cinematic. No people, no text.\n\n---------\nC#5. \n\nOne continuous shot, 15 seconds, no cuts. The very first frame is exactly the starting image at its full normal brightness. No fade-in, no black screen, no pause at the start: the action begins instantly and violently on the first frame. The last frame matches the ending image exactly.\n\nCRITICAL: The burial mound in front of the camera keeps exactly the same shape and position. The constellations in the sky match the shapes and positions in the ending image exactly. The rising cap and shoes are clean, crisp, solid objects at all times: no trails, no afterimages, no ghosting, no motion echoes, no duplicated copies behind them.\n\nCOLOR AND EXPOSURE: all light in this shot is a rich, warm gold and amber color. No white flash, no white glow, no blown-out highlights, no overexposure. The cap and shoes always keep their golden color and visible metal details; they never turn into white light.\n\n0-1s: explosive launch. On the very first frame, the glowing mound right in front of the camera erupts in a sudden, powerful burst of warm golden light. A shockwave bursts outward, blasting the mist away and whipping the grass around the mound. In the same instant, two gleaming gilt-bronze artifacts shoot straight up out of the mound's top like rockets, at tremendous speed, their polished gold surfaces glowing warmly: first a tall pointed Baekje cap with two small cup-shaped ornaments on top, then a split second later a pair of long, low slipper-shaped openwork shoes. They leave the mound within half a second, sharp and fast.\n\n1-3.5s: fast tilt up. The camera whips upward in a very fast, energetic tilt, chasing the golden cap and shoes as they rocket into the sky. The glowing mound and the bare winter trees behind it flash through the frame and drop away, until only a thin border of dark, leafless twigs remains along the edges of the frame, as in the ending image, and the frame is filled with the dark, realistic night sky and the faint Milky Way. The camera eases to a stop and stays still from here to the end.\n\n3.5-9s: main constellations. High in the sky, the golden cap and shoes dissolve into starlight: small golden star points appear along their outlines and thin, soft golden lines connect them one by one, drawing the cap as a constellation at the center of the sky and the pair of shoes as a constellation to its right, exactly as in the ending image. The rest of the sky is still empty of constellations.\n\n9-14s: the whole sky. One by one, the other constellations are drawn around them in thin, soft golden lines, exactly as in the ending image: the dagger at the upper left, the stirrup at the left, the stemmed cup at the lower left, the jar at the top center and the ewer at the top right, and the pair of stirrups at the lower right.\n\n14-15s: the finished celestial map glows softly, all constellations gently twinkling like distant starlight, matching the ending image.\n\nThe constellations are thin, distant golden starlight in the sky, never solid objects. Majestic, sacred, awe-inspiring, cinematic, photorealistic night sky. No people, no text.",
